    "cover_image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1490730141103-6cac27aaab94?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80",
    "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/34994",
    "days": [
      {
        "day_number": 1,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 1) (1)",
        "content": "<p>Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week</p><p>By DR. JOHN HABERMANN. Together With</p><p>Confessional, Communion, and Other Prayers and Hymns for Mornings and Evenings, and Other Occasions</p><p>Done in English By EMIL H. RAUSCH. Editor Lutheran Herald</p><p>Chicago, Illinois. WARTBURG PUBLISHING HOUSE.</p><p>Translator’s Preface</p><p>This little manual of prayers herewith offered to English speaking Christians in their own language, has long been one of the treasures of the German people. With the exception of a few prayers, as hereinafter noted, it was originally written by one of God’s noblemen, by one who “lived and moved and had his being” in the things of the Kingdom of God. Dr. John Habermann (known also as Avenarius, Latinized form of Habermann) died 1590 as superintendent at Zeitz, was a famous preacher and a distinguished scholar of his day. He was noted for his profound knowledge of oriental languages especially of the Hebrew. Still it is not this but the fact of his little prayer book that has endeared him to his fellow Christians. And this manual of prayers is the mature product of an inner life rich in the grace of God. On every page it bears the stamp of one for whom the communion with the eternal Father in heaven through the faith in Jesus Christ, the Savior, is a blessed reality. Nothing more natural therefore also than that he should “live and move and have his being” in the language of the Word of God. And this is quite apparent in his prayer language. God’s Word give him the terms to express his thoughts. Especially the Psalter, the prayer and hymn book of Israel, proves a veritable thesaurus of prayer terms and of these he makes a copious use.</p><p>The present little volume presents the Englished edition of “Wachet und Betet,” as issued by the Synod of Iowa and other States. Owing to the exigencies of the times, with the great world war raging in all its fury, a special set of prayers for times of war has been added by the translator, in the hope that they will add to the usefulness of the book. These are found on pages 131-138 . The hymns as far as possible are given in the form as found in the new Common Service Book with Hymnal. Many of them however are new translations that here appear in print for the first time. For these we are indebted especially to Prof. Alfred Ramsey of the Lutheran Theological Seminary, Maywood, Chicago, and the Rev. H. Brueckner of Iowa City, Iowa, a fact which is here gratefully acknowledged.</p><p>The labor of clothing these little gems of prayer into the language of the land has been done as a labor of love, albeit the stress of other work often precluded the continued effort. The work was done a bit at a time. This little volume is herewith issued with the fervent hope and prayer, that it may long continue on its course of blessing, and lead many lives into the closer communion with God, through Jesus Christ. Soli Deo Gloria!</p><p>E. H. R.</p><p>Waverly, Iowa, during the blessed season of Epiphany, 1918.</p><p>EXHORTATION TO PRAYER</p><p>Arise, dear soul, and carefully reflect who He is with whom you speak and before whom you stand when you pray. See, you speak with God, your Maker, and stand in the presence of Him, the eternal Majesty, whom thousand times thousand holy angels and arch-angels attend. Therefore, O Christian, enter you into the closet of your soul, and beware, lest you fail to put from you all sluggishness of heart, and lift up to your God a countenance free from blame. Then will you delight in the Lord and have power with Him, and prevail. Yes, you will conquer the unconquerable God and bear away the blessing through Jesus Christ. Amen.</p><p>The Lord’s Prayer</p><p>Our Father, who are in heaven; Hallowed be Your Name; Your kingdom come; Your will be done on earth as it is in heaven; Give us this day our daily bread; And forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us; And lead us not into temptation; but deliver us from evil; For Yours is the kingdom, and the power, and the glory, forever and ever. Amen.</p><p>The Benediction</p><p>The Lord bless you, and keep you.</p><p>The Lord make His face to shine upon you,</p><p>and be gracious to you.</p><p>The Lord lift up His countenance upon you,</p><p>and give you peace. Amen.</p><p>Morning and Evening Prayers</p><p>Prayer for Sunday Morning.</p><p>Lord, our Heavenly Father, Eternal God! Blessed be Your divine power and might; magnified Your fathomless goodness and mercy; praised Your eternal wisdom and truth. For You have shielded me with Your hand against the perils of this night, and have suffered me to rest and slumber in peace under the shadow of Your wings. You have kept and safeguarded me with a father’s care against the Evil One and all his wicked designs and purposes. Therefore, I magnify Your goodness and the wonders which You do for the children of men. I will exalt You in the congregation. Your praise shall forever be in my mouth. My soul shall bless You, O my Lord, all that is within me shall bless Your holy name, and nevermore will I forget Your benefits. May the praises of my lips, which in singleness of heart I bring before You at this early hour, be acceptable in Your sight. I call upon You with all my heart to preserve me this day against all danger of body and soul. May Your holy angels have charge over me and keep me in all my ways. Encompass me with Your shield and lead me on the paths of Your commandments that, like the children of light, I may be blameless in Your service, to Your good pleasure. Stay the Evil One and all wickedness of this world. Restrain my own flesh and blood that I be not overcome by them. Lead me with Your Holy Spirit that I attempt, do, speak, or think nothing except what is well-pleasing in Your sight and conducive to the glory of Your divine Majesty. See, O God, I consecrate and dedicate myself entirely to Your holy will, with body and soul, all my powers and abilities, inwardly and outwardly. Make me a living sacrifice, holy and acceptable to You, so that I may render You a reasonable and pleasing service. Therefore, Most Holy Father, Almighty God, let me be wholly Your. Govern You my heart and soul, and all my emotions that I know and understand none but You. O Lord, in the morning Will you hear my voice. Early will I seek You and look up to You. Early will I praise You, and will not cease when evening comes. Through Jesus Christ. Amen.</p><p>Gott des Himmels und der Erden.</p><p>God, Who made earth and heaven,—</p><p>Father, Son, and Holy Spirit,</p><p>Who the day and night have given,</p><p>Sun and moon, and starry host,</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 6:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 2,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 1) (2)",
        "content": "<p>You Whose mighty hand sustains</p><p>Earth and all that it contains;</p><p>Praise to You my soul shall render,</p><p>Who this night has guarded me,</p><p>My omnipotent Defender,</p><p>Who from ill does set me free;</p><p>Free from danger, anguish, woe,</p><p>Free from the infernal foe.</p><p>Let the night of my transgression</p><p>With night’s darkness pass away;</p><p>Jesus, into Your possession</p><p>I resign myself today.</p><p>In Your wounds I find relief</p><p>From my greatest sin and grief.</p><p>Let my life and conversation</p><p>Be directed by Your Word;</p><p>Lord, Your constant preservation</p><p>To Your erring child afford.</p><p>Nowhere but alone in You</p><p>From all harm can I be free.</p><p>Wholly to Your blest protection</p><p>I commit my heart and mind;</p><p>Mighty God! to Your direction</p><p>Wholly may I be resigned.</p><p>Lord, my Shield, my Light divine,</p><p>O accept, and own me Your.</p><p>Heinrich Albert, 1643.</p><p>Tr. John Christian Jacobi, 1720.</p><p>Arthur Tozer Russell, 1848.</p><p>Catherine Winkworth, 1855.</p><p>Prayer for Sunday Evening.</p><p>Eternal God, Merciful Father, I lift up my hands to You as an evening sacrifice, and render You most hearty thanks, praise, and glory, that You have protected me this day and all the days of my life from all evil and calamity, and through the ministrations of Your holy angels have graciously guarded me against the Evil One. I ask you to forgive me all my sins, wherever I have done wrong. Surround me this night with Your holy angels. May you compass me round and cast a trench about me, that I may escape the snares and evil cunning of the enemy. I commend myself to Your goodness and mercy. Protect me with Your outstretched arm; for from my heart do I pant after You in the nightwatches, and with my spirit within me do I watch for You at all times. I wait upon Your goodness, and my soul trusts in You, the living God, for You are my refuge and my Savior. See, O Lord, whether we sleep or wake, we Are yours: whether we live or die, You are our God who has called us into being. Therefore, I cry to You: let Your grace be not far from me. Shelter me with Your shield. Keep me, that I lie in quiet, sleep in peace, and awake again in health. Hide me in Your pavilion in the time of trouble, in the secret of Your tabernacle hide me, set me upon the rock, and I will fear no evil. Yes, though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil: for You are with me; Your rod and Your staff they comfort me. Grant me grace, that though my body sleep, my soul may ever wake for You, that I may ever have You in my heart and before my mind, and be not overcome by the night of sin. Keep me from all wicked and evil dreams, from restless wakings and useless anxieties, from depraved and hurtful thoughts, from all grief. My Lord and God, into Your gracious care do I commend my body and soul, my brothers and sisters, and all my kin and loved ones. May it please You, O Lord, to save us, and turn not Your mercy from us. May Your grace and fidelity protect us always. Cover us this night with Your goodness, and encompass us with Your mercy, that we be safe-guarded in body and soul. Amen.</p><p>Christe, du bist der helle Tag.</p><p>O Christ, who are the sun-lit day,</p><p>Before You night must flee away,</p><p>You do reflect the Father’s light</p><p>And teachest us His will aright.</p><p>Dear Lord, as night is drawing near,</p><p>Fill You our hearts with light and cheer,</p><p>Let us securely rest in You</p><p>And from the foe’s attacks stay free.</p><p>And while our eyes in slumber close,</p><p>Grant that our hearts may find repose;</p><p>But let them be to You awake</p><p>And of Your saving grace partake.</p><p>Protect us from the wily foe</p><p>Who seeks to harm our souls, we know.</p><p>Be You our shield, our staff, and stay,</p><p>Lord Jesus Christ, for this we pray.</p><p>For You have made us, Lord, Your own,</p><p>We as Your heritage are known.</p><p>Your blood was shed, that we might be</p><p>The Father’s precious gift to You.</p><p>So let Your holy angel stay</p><p>Around us both by night and day.</p><p>Place You a watch beside our bed,</p><p>And guardian angels overhead.</p><p>Thus in Your name we fall asleep,</p><p>While angels o’er us watch must keep.</p><p>To You, O Holy One in Three,</p><p>Be praise to all eternity.</p><p>Latin, 7 Century.</p><p>Erasmus Alber, 1555.</p><p>Tr. H. Brueckner, 1918.</p><p>Prayer for Monday Morning.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 23:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 3,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 2) (1)",
        "content": "<p>O You Eternal and Merciful God! You have commanded Your people in Your law each morning to offer You a burnt offering, by it to praise and thank You for Your merciful protection: thus I too would bring to You my offering of praise, that is the fruit of my lips, and magnify Your holy name. For by Your grace and mercy You have kept me this night from all evil and harm in body and soul, and have graciously protected me. If You had not been my shield and my help, manifold calamities would have engulfed me, and I could not have risen in health and safety. Therefore, I thank You for Your protection. But I continue to call to You from the depths of my heart, and my supplication ascends to You in the early hour. Early do I seek Your countenance and Ask you to safeguard me and mine from the wiles and power of the devil, from sin and disgrace and all wickedness. Visit You me in this early hour with Your grace, as without You I can do nothing, and grant that I may this day begin all my work in Your name and end it joyously, to the glory of Your divine majesty and the betterment of my neighbor. Preserve my soul, mind, reason, senses, and thoughts, all that I do and leave undone, that the prince of darkness do me no injury. Safeguard me against the destruction that wastes at noonday. Defend me against my enemies that neither secretly nor openly they harm or injure me with their craft and cunning, violence or malice. O God, Father and Lord of my life, shield me from all impurity and disorderly conduct. Keep me from all intemperance and unchastity, and turn from me shameless thoughts. Help me by Your grace to pluck out the eye that offends me and cast it away, and renounce all wicked and impure desires of the heart. Grant whatever is pleasing to You and useful to me, that I may serve You in the true faith. Look upon me with the eyes of Your mercy, You Savior of the world, and enlighten my heart and eyes, that I may walk in the light of Your grace, which rises above me, and never lose You, the Eternal Light. Amen.</p><p>Ach bleib mit deiner Gnade.</p><p>Abide with grace unbounded,</p><p>Lord Jesus, with us still,</p><p>That Satan’s craft confounded</p><p>May no more work us ill.</p><p>Abide with us, dear Savior,</p><p>Both with and in Your Word:</p><p>To us both now and ever</p><p>Your saving health afford.</p><p>Abide with all Your brightness,</p><p>You brightest Light of all;</p><p>And lest we stray from rightness,</p><p>Make You Your truth our wall.</p><p>Abide with us and bless us,</p><p>You Lord whose riches ’bide;</p><p>With growing grace possess us</p><p>And all things best provide.</p><p>Abide with Your protection,</p><p>Great Captain, clothed with might;</p><p>O’ercome our world affection</p><p>And vanquish Satan’s spite.</p><p>Abide with care untiring,</p><p>Our God and Lord indeed;</p><p>All steadfastness inspiring,</p><p>Help, Lord, our every need.</p><p>Josua Stegmann, 1630.</p><p>Tr. A. Ramsey, 1916.</p><p>Prayer for Monday Evening.</p><p>O You Mighty and Everlasting God, the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, I thank You that by Your divine power You have this day preserved me from all injury and danger of body and life. I owe it to Your mercy alone that You did protect me on all my paths. I ask you to forgive me all my sins which I have committed against You, and this night and during our entire lives mercifully to defend me and my loved ones against all sorrow and anxiety, and against the craft and power of the devil, with which day and night he seeks to ensnare us. Preserve us from the deadly pestilence that walks in the darkness, and deliver us from the snare of the enemy. Protect us from the temptation and terror of Satan, from all evils of body and soul. For You are our strong fortress, our sword and buckler. All our hope and trust rests in You. Therefore, O faithful God, may Your eyes be upon us and we be safeguarded this night against all violence and assault of the enemy. Be You our keeper and protector. Encompass us with Your shelter, for in You is our salvation. To You only, from whom comes my help, do I lift up my eyes. My help comes from the Lord, who made heaven and earth. See, as the eyes of servants look to the hands of their masters, and as the eyes of a maiden to the hand of her mistress; so our eyes wait upon the Lord our God, until that He have mercy upon us. Have mercy upon us, O Lord, have mercy, for we are poor and needy. Lift upon me the light of Your countenance lest I sleep the sleep of death. Amen.</p><p>Nun ruhen alle Waelder.</p><p>Now under night’s dark shadow</p><p>Rest woodland, field, and meadow,</p><p>The world in slumber lies.</p><p>But you, my soul, awake you,</p><p>To song and prayer betake you,</p><p>Give praise to Him who rules the skies.</p><p>The sun’s fair light has vanished,</p><p>The night its rays has banished,</p><p>The night, the foe of day.</p><p>’Tis well: my heart contains</p><p>A sun whose light ne’er wanes,</p><p>Since Jesus there holds constant sway.</p><p>The reign of day is over,</p><p>And golden stars now cover</p><p>The canopy so blue.</p><p>Thus I shall shine in heaven,</p><p>Where golden crowns are given</p><p>To all who faithful stay and true.</p><p>My body is divested</p><p>Of garments that have rested</p><p>Upon its form of clay.</p><p>Thus I at heaven’s portal</p><p>Shall lose all that is mortal</p><p>And with the Lord forever stay.</p><p>Head, feet, and hands are taking</p><p>Sweet rest from toil and waking,</p><p>Released from ev’ry pain.</p><p>O heart of mine, why borrow</p><p>The troubles of tomorrow?</p><p>You rest from sin and woe will gain.</p><p>You members weak and tired,</p><p>By joy no more inspired,</p><p>Betake yourselves to bed.</p><p>The time and hour for sleeping</p><p>In God’s own faithful keeping</p><p>Will come when you are cold and dead.</p><p>My tired eyes are closing,</p><p>And while I am reposing,</p><p>Where does my soul remain?</p><p>To You be it commended</p><p>Until the night is ended,</p><p>Let me Your gracious favor gain.</p><p>Lord Jesus, who do love me,</p><p>Spread both Your wings above me,</p><p>Thus shielding me from harm.</p><p>If Satan should draw near me,</p><p>Let angels come to cheer me</p><p>And so the wily foe disarm.</p><p>My loved ones, rest securely,</p><p>Since God will guard you surely</p><p>From pain and perils sore.</p><p>May you in safety slumber,</p><p>While angels without number</p><p>Attend you now and forever.</p><p>Paul Gerhardt, 1648.</p><p>Tr. H. Brueckner, 1918.</p><p>Prayer for Tuesday Morning.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 123:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 4,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 2) (2)",
        "content": "<p>Blessed be God, the maker of heaven and earth, blessed be the Lord, who only does wondrous things, and blessed be His glorious name forever who has made both day and night through His glorious wisdom, and so ruled, that while the earth remains, they shall not cease, that man may rest by night, and proceed again to his labors by day. O Lord, how manifold are Your works! In wisdom Have you made them all: the earth is full of Your riches. For such Your gifts we should thank You before the sun rises, and come into Your presence when the light breaks forth. Therefore I bless You now also, for You have suffered me securely to rest and sleep this night, and again awakened me to the joy of living. You have mercifully sheltered me from the assaults and malice of enemies. I supplicate Your grace: grant that my soul may repose in Your hands, my body continue in health according to Your good pleasure, and be kept from all injury and peril. Be You my mighty protection and strong stay, a defence from heat, and my cover from the sun at noon, my preservation from stumbling, and my help from falling, that no harm may come to me. O Merciful God, knowing the hour is come to awaken out of the sleep of sin and iniquity, for now is our salvation so near, the night is far spent, the day is at hand: so help us to cast off the works of darkness, and put on the armour of light, that we may walk honestly as in the day; not in rioting and drunkenness, not in chambering and wantonness, not in strife and envying, but putting on the Lord Jesus Christ in the true faith and a Christian conduct. Thus awaken us each morning. Open You my ears that I may hear Your holy Word with a believing heart and keep the same in my memory. Let my ears incline to the entreaties and prayers of the needy, not to forsake them in their distress. And when in my distress I cry to You, hear You the voice of my supplications, and despise not my sighings in the hour of death. Let my prayers come before You early. Incline Your ears to my entreaties. Satisfy us early with Your mercy; that we may rejoice and be glad all our days. Amen.</p><p>Wach auf, mein Herz, und singe.</p><p>Awake, my heart, rejoicing,</p><p>Your Maker’s praises voicing,—</p><p>The Giver, good gifts sending;</p><p>Their Shield, His folk defending.</p><p>All night while darkness ’bound me</p><p>In deepest gloom around me,</p><p>By Satan craved while sleeping</p><p>God had me in His keeping.</p><p>You spak’st me words endearing;</p><p>Sleep now, my child, unfearing;</p><p>Sleep well, night’s terrors spurning;</p><p>You’lt see the sun returning.</p><p>Your word performed, now waking,</p><p>I see the bright dawn breaking,</p><p>Safe kept from ills unnumbered</p><p>While ’neath Your care I slumbered.</p><p>An off’ring You desire.</p><p>See what You require.</p><p>Nor lamb nor incense bringing,</p><p>I come with prayer and singing.</p><p>Nor Will you now despise them,</p><p>But in Your heart will prize them,</p><p>Well knowing, yes, and surely</p><p>My best I offer purely.</p><p>Approve my works when shown You.</p><p>Help You good councils only;</p><p>Beginning, middle, closing,</p><p>Lord, for the best disposing.</p><p>With blessings guard me waking,</p><p>My heart Your dwelling making,</p><p>And with Your Word, Lord, feed me</p><p>While heavenward You do lead me.</p><p>Paul Gerhardt, 1648.</p><p>Tr. A. Ramsey, 1916.</p><p>Prayer for Tuesday Evening.</p><p>Lord, Merciful God, Holy Father, in the daytime do I cry to You with my voice, in my distress I call upon You, and at eventime I remember Your goodness and mercy, which You have wrought for me. And especially do I magnify You now, that purely out of fatherly grace and mercy, without any merit or worthiness on my part, You have this day preserved me from all harm and danger and kept me from sudden death. Therefore do I now and at all times render to You praise and thanksgiving, and Ask you, for the sake of the bitter sufferings of Jesus Christ, to forgive me wherever I have sinned against You this day. Mercifully protect me during the night against my adversary, the devil, and against the fears and terrors of the night. Suffer me to rest without anxieties and worries, and may the eyes of my faith ever see the lustre of Your countenance even during the shades of night. For You who shining and true light, which dispels all darkness that surrounds us. You, O Lord, art ever with me. You are my rock, and my fortress, my deliverer, my strength, in whom I will trust, my buckler, the horn of my salvation, and my high tower. Lord, my God, at eventime do I lift up my hands to You. Come to me as the latter rains that make the earth fruitful. Abide with us, for the day is far spent and in the darkness there is none to defend us save You alone, our God. Hasten to uphold us. Defend us this night, lest our souls fall into the sleep of sin and our bodies be overcome with evil. Awake us again in due time, and make us to hear joy and peace, for we love Your word and Your testimonies, which are the delight of our souls. May our ears be saved from all messages of sorrow, and all anguish be turned from our souls; for You can prosper all that lives, and fill my life with Your blessing; in Jesus Christ, our Lord. Amen.</p><p>Die Nacht ist kommen, drin wir ruhen sollen.</p><p>Now God be with us, for the night is closing;</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 13:13"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 5,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 3) (1)",
        "content": "<p>The light and darkness are of His disposing;</p><p>And ’neath His shadow here to rest we yield us,</p><p>For He will shield us.</p><p>Let evil thoughts and spirits flee before us;</p><p>Till morning comes, watch, O Master, o’er us;</p><p>In soul and body You from harm defend us,</p><p>Your angels send us.</p><p>Let holy thoughts be ours when sleep o’ertakes us;</p><p>Our earliest thoughts be Your when morning wakes us;</p><p>All day serve You, in all that we are doing</p><p>Your praise pursuing.</p><p>As Your beloved, soothe the sick and weeping,</p><p>And bid the prisoner lose his griefs in sleeping;</p><p>Widows and orphans, we to You commend them;</p><p>Do You befriend them.</p><p>We have no refuge, none on earth to aid us,</p><p>Save You, O Father, Who Your own have made us;</p><p>But Your dear presence will not leave them lonely,</p><p>Who seek You only.</p><p>Father, Your name be praised, Your kingdom given;</p><p>Your will be done on earth as ’tis in heaven;</p><p>Keep us in life, forgive our sins, deliver</p><p>Us now and ever.</p><p>Petrus Herbert, 1566.</p><p>St. 5, Soon., 1627.</p><p>Tr. Catherine Winkworth, 1863.</p><p>Prayer for Wednesday Morning.</p><p>Almighty, All-gracious God! All Your creatures should praise and glorify You. The birds under the heavens magnify You with lovely songs early in the morning as their Lord and Maker. So will I too heartily thank You, that You have preserved me under Your shelter and protection during the night now past, and all my life even to the present hour, and awakening me from the sleep of the darkness of this night, have suffered me to arise again in health and joy. I ask you for the sake of the saving resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead, that You would ever keep me together with all my loved ones from all danger and evil. O Lord, save Your people, and bless Your inheritance: feed them also, and lift them up forever. Fill me also at this early hour with Your grace, that I may pass this day rejoicing in Your commandments, and free from mortal sin. Let me experience Your grace as a dew from the womb of the morning, and as the refreshing moisture that diffuses at the break of day, making the land fruitful. You would spread Your goodness over me, that I may gladly and zealously do Your will. Govern me with Your Holy Spirit that I may serve You in righteousness and holiness of truth, well pleasing in Your sight. Guard me that I sin not against You, nor defile my conscience with carnal lusts that militate against the soul. Keep my tongue from evil, and my lips from speaking guile. Foolish talking or jesting, unbecoming of Christians, be ever far removed from me. Grant, that I offend none with my lips, nor backbite, judge nor condemn, defame nor vilify. O that I might put a lock to my lips and seal them with a strong seal, that they bring me not to nothing, nor my tongue destroy me. Give me grace that I may know my shortcomings and correct them, and not fall into Your righteous judgment and condemnation. Grant my prayer, O Eternal God, for the sake of Your dear Son, Jesus Christ. Amen.</p><p>Morgenglanz der Ewigkeit.</p><p>Dayspring of Eternity,</p><p>Light from endless Light proceeding,</p><p>Let Your beams upon us shine</p><p>As the shadows are receding;</p><p>And dispel by Your great might</p><p>Our dark night.</p><p>As the soft refreshing dew</p><p>Falls upon the drooping flower,</p><p>So our fainting hearts renew</p><p>By Your Spirit’s quickening power;</p><p>Ne’er Your bounteous grace withhold</p><p>From Your fold.</p><p>Let the glow of Your pure love</p><p>All our icy coldness banish;</p><p>In the radiance from above</p><p>May our doubts and fears all vanish,</p><p>That before dying we may be</p><p>Found in You.</p><p>O You glorious Sun of grace,</p><p>May Your light be ne’er denied us!</p><p>Till we reach the heavenly place</p><p>Shine upon our way to guide us,</p><p>That at last among the blest</p><p>We may rest.</p><p>Christian Knorr v. Rosenroth, 1684.</p><p>Tr. J. F. Ohl, 1915.</p><p>Prayer for Wednesday Evening.</p><p>Most Holy Trinity, One in essence, Three in person, who are my life, salvation, and eternal joy, I praise and thank You with mouth and heart that You have so graciously protected me throughout this day. I pray Your divine goodness to cover up all my shortcomings, and especially where this day, with my tongue, with vain and unprofitable words, slander or otherwise, I have sinned against You and Your holy commandments. According to Your name, O God, so is Your praise to the ends of the earth: Your right hand is full of righteousness. Therefore I commend my body and my soul into Your hands. Your divine Majesty bless me; Your holy Trinity shelter me; Your eternal Unity preserve me. May Your unbounded mercy protect me; Your inexpressible benevolence defend me, the sublime truth of God cover me; profound knowledge of Christ strengthen me; the unfathomable goodness of the Lord keep me. The grace of the Father govern me; the wisdom of the Son refresh me; the power of the Holy Spirit enlighten me. My Creator aid me, my Redeemer quicken me, my Comforter abide with me. The Lord bless me and keep me. The Lord make His face shine upon me and be gracious to me. The Lord lift up His countenance upon me and give me peace. The protection and blessing of the one and eternal Godhead be between me and all my enemies, visible and invisible, today and always, that they may not approach nor injure me. As the pillar of the cloud went between the army of the Egyptians and the army of Israel, so that they could not come together, and no harm befall the children of Israel, so May you be a pillar of fire and a wall of separation between me and my enemies, that no harm come over me. Keep me also in my last hour. When my eyes no longer see, my ears no longer hear, my tongue no longer speak, be You with me, O blessed Trinity, that the Evil One have no power over me. Amen.</p><p>Hirte deiner Schafe.</p><p>Shepherd never sleeping,</p><p>In Your gracious keeping</p><p>I have been today.</p><p>You are my Defender,</p><p>So in mercy tender</p><p>Come and with me stay;</p><p>All this night</p><p>Keep me in sight,</p><p>Send Your angels to attend me</p><p>And protection lend me.</p><p>While I lie and slumber,</p><p>Let Your hosts outnumber</p><p>All my raging foes.</p><p>Be of grace the Giver,</p><p>And Your child deliver</p><p>From guilt’s painful throes.</p><p>For Your Son</p><p>My soul has won;</p><p>By His wounds, so sorely stricken,</p><p>He my heart does quicken.</p><p>Shield You from all danger</p><p>Ev’ry lonely stranger</p><p>And my dear ones, too.</p><p>Tenderly embrace us</p><p>And with mercy grace us,</p><p>Be our Father true.</p><p>You with me</p><p>And I with You,</p><p>Thus shall I, mine eyelids closing,</p><p>Be in peace reposing.</p><p>Close the door behind us,</p><p>Let no evil find us,</p><p>Keep all ills away.</p><p>Be our shield and cover,</p><p>Let Your angels hover</p><p>O’er us now, we pray.</p><p>By sweet rest</p><p>Let us be blest,</p><p>Ev’ry fear of Satan’s raging</p><p>In our hearts assuaging.</p><p>What if death should take me</p><p>And no light awake me</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 28:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 6,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 3) (2)",
        "content": "<p>From my sleep and rest?</p><p>If You have intended</p><p>That my life be ended,</p><p>Let Your name be blest;</p><p>As for me,</p><p>I yield to You.</p><p>In the wounds of Jesus lying,</p><p>I am daily dying.</p><p>By no cares encumbered,</p><p>Though my hours be numbered,</p><p>I now fall asleep.</p><p>All to You commending</p><p>Who Your hosts are sending</p><p>Watch o’er me to keep.</p><p>Through the night</p><p>Be my delight,</p><p>And if I should see the morrow,</p><p>You will cure all sorrow.</p><p>Benjamin Schmolcke, 1715.</p><p>Tr. H. Brueckner, 1918.</p><p>Prayer for Thursday Morning.</p><p>Jesus Christ, You are the eternal light, which dispels the darkness of night and the shadow of death: I magnify Your name, I glorify and thank You. For You have so graciously kept me during this night, and have brought me out of the darkness to the light of day. You have shielded me against the terrors of the night, the snares of the devil, the noisome pestilence, that walks in the darkness, manifold illness and disease. You have guarded and watched over my soul, even as the shepherd watches over his flock. And all that I possess is kept from harm through Your great mercy. Praise and thanks be said to You for Your gracious protection and all Your gifts. I will speak of Your power and magnify Your goodness, when the day breaks. For You are my refuge, my strong tower, my present help, my faithful God, in whom I trust. You make glad my heart and my countenance rejoices. I ask you, for the sake of Your holy birth and incarnation, suffer Your grace to rise in my heart and break forth even as the beauty of the morning, and come over me as the early rain. Illumine me with Your radiance, and be You the light of my heart, for You are the right day star and the true light, that lights men to the eternal life. Be merciful to me, O Lord, for in You do I put my trust. My soul waits for You, more than they that watch for the morning, yes more than they that watch for the morning. Be You mine arm in the morning, my salvation also in the time of trouble. Protect me in body and soul, that no evil befall me and no plague come near to my dwelling. Keep from me all wicked spirits. Defend me from evildoers. Stand up for me against the workers of iniquity and shield me, that the hands of mine adversaries may not touch me. O Lord, our God, establish You the work of our hands upon us; yes, the work of our hands establish You it, and strengthen our hands, and teach us that we may keep Your commandments and sin not against You this day. Grant us this for the sake of Your mercy, which endures forever and ever. Amen.</p><p>Wach auf, mein Herz, die Nacht ist hin.</p><p>Awake, my soul; the rising sun</p><p>Dispels the night of mourning;</p><p>Awake, with songs of praises run</p><p>To greet the Lord returning.</p><p>He burst the gates of death today</p><p>And left the gloomy grave for aye</p><p>While all the world rejoices.</p><p>Arise, my soul, from sin and death,</p><p>To you new life is given;</p><p>Arise and run the race of faith;</p><p>Fix your desires on heaven</p><p>Where Jesus, your Redeemer reigns,</p><p>And seek the things that it contains,</p><p>If you with Him be risen.</p><p>Are you distressed by weight of care?</p><p>Your Savior will remove it.</p><p>Believing, you with joy can bear</p><p>Your cross and learn to love it.</p><p>Cast all your burden on the Lord;</p><p>Fear not; for He will help afford,</p><p>For now He has arisen.</p><p>Now Judah’s Lion, true and tried,</p><p>The victory obtains;</p><p>The Lamb of God, the Crucified,</p><p>For us salvation gains,</p><p>And gives righteousness and life;</p><p>For after all the dreadful strife,</p><p>O’er every foe He triumphs.</p><p>Then up, my soul, begin the fight,</p><p>For Christ, the Victor, leads.</p><p>He arms you with a victor’s might;</p><p>With Him your cause succeeds.</p><p>Now you can’st rise and live anew</p><p>And righteousness and peace pursue</p><p>And be a faithful servant.</p><p>Fear not the angry jaws of hell,</p><p>Nor world, nor death, nor devil.</p><p>Your Savior lives and all is well,</p><p>Though sore has been His travail.</p><p>A Victor crowned, He as a Friend</p><p>The mean and feeble does attend,</p><p>And therefore you will conquer.</p><p>Ah, Lord, whom death could not defile,</p><p>Who from the dead have risen,</p><p>Free us from Satan’s might and guile</p><p>And save us from his prison.</p><p>O grant, that, as one body, we</p><p>May enter that new life in You</p><p>Which You for us have gotten.</p><p>Laurentius Laurenti, 1700.</p><p>Tr. A. Ramsey.</p><p>Prayer for Thursday Evening.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 94:16"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 7,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 4) (1)",
        "content": "<p>Praise be to You, O God, our Father, through Jesus Christ in the Holy Spirit, one, eternal God, who through Your manifold compassion have kept me this day, a poor sinner and miserable creature, from the fiery darts of Satan that fly by day, from the destruction that wastes at noonday, and have graciously protected me from a sudden and evil death. Your mercy, O Lord, is in the heavens; and Your faithfulness reaches to the clouds. You are gracious and merciful, and all Your acts are glorious. I ask you, O merciful God, graciously to forgive me all that I have done against You this day, in thought, word, or deed. Turn Your mercy toward me, that I may slumber and rest during this coming night, and that I may never turn from You, who are the eternal rest. Suffer me ever to abide in You in the true faith, and safely to sojourn under Your protection, so that the enemy may never come near to me, nor do me injury. Lord, You are my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear? You are the strength of my life; of whom shall I be afraid? My heart trusts in You, and I am helped. You are my strength and my great shield. Your right hand strengthens me. Your right hand gladdens my heart, and in the shadow of Your wings will I make my refuge. See, my God, in the daytime do I cry to You, and You hear me, and in the night season I am not silent, and You answers my prayer. I remember You on my bed, and meditate on You in the night watches, because You have been my help. Therefore in the shadow of Your wings will I rejoice. My soul cleaves to You, for Your right hand defends me. When darkness comes over me, then You, O Lord, art my light and my salvation. O gracious God, vouchsafe to me Your grace, so that when that last hour comes, and I lay me down on my deathbed for the eternal rest, through Your help, in the true faith, in all confidence and trust, I may happily fall asleep for the eternal life. Meanwhile keep me in You, that I may ever watch and pass my days in all sobriety and moderation, and be found in Christian readiness, since I can not know the hour when You come, O God, to call me hence, so that I may be worthy to stand before the Son of Man, and be not put to shame; who lives and reigns with You and the Holy Spirit, world without end. Amen.</p><p>Nur in Jesu Blut und Wunden.</p><p>Now in Jesus’ wounds reposing,</p><p>I my tired eyes am closing.</p><p>For His love and pardoning grace</p><p>Are my only resting place.</p><p>Through the day His mercy holds me,</p><p>And by night His arm enfolds me.</p><p>Of Your strong protection sure,</p><p>Jesus, I shall rest secure.</p><p>Tr. H. Brueckner, 1916.</p><p>Prayer for Friday Morning.</p><p>Blessed be God, my Maker! Blessed be God, my Savior! Blessed be God, my Comforter! Who gives to me my health, my life, and every blessing; my very present help and my protection. You have kept me according to Your great and most blessed compassion during this night now past against the onslaughts of Satan, and preserved me in health. I urge you, Heavenly Father, through Jesus Christ, Your dear Son, take me this day also into Your divine protection, and shield me that no evil may assail my life. For into Your hands do I this day and all days commend my body and soul, my thoughts, words, and deeds, all that I do or leave undone, my going out and my coming in, my walks and ways, my rising up and my lying down, my will and counsel, my thoughts and desires, my faith and profession, the end of my life, the day and hour of my death, my passing away and my resurrection. O Lord God, do You with me as You will: for You know what serves best Your glory and my salvation. Keep me in Your fear and in the true knowledge of You. Protect me from the deeds of unrighteousness. And if perhaps and by reason of my frailty I sin against You, I ask you take not from me Your mercy; turn not from me Your grace; withdraw not Your help. For there is none other God nor Helper but You, and as there is none before You, there is none after You. You are the first and the last, Alpha and Omega, and there is none other God beside You. Therefore do I call only upon You: may Your goodness rule over me. Cause me to hear Your loving kindness in the morning; for in You do I trust. Lead me on the paths of righteousness, that I may not walk in the counsel of the ungodly, nor stand in the way of sinners, nor yet sit in the seat of the scornful, but that my heart may ever delight in Your word and commandments, and meditate upon them day and night; through Jesus Christ, our Lord. Amen.</p><p>Die helle Sonn leucht jetzt herfuer.</p><p>The morning sun shines in the skies,</p><p>And we from peaceful slumbers rise.</p><p>All praise to God who has this night</p><p>Protected us from Satan’s might.</p><p>Lord Jesus, shield us now by day</p><p>From sin and error on our way.</p><p>To us Your holy angels send,</p><p>And let them to our wants attend.</p><p>Make You our hearts obedient,</p><p>To use Your word and sacrament,</p><p>To do Your will whate’er betide,</p><p>Thus pleasing You, our trusty guide.</p><p>Bless You the labor of our hands</p><p>And help us keep Your law’s demands,</p><p>That all our work, begun in You,</p><p>May to Your praise and glory be.</p><p>Nicolaus Hermann, 1560.</p><p>Tr. H. Brueckner, 1918.</p><p>Prayer for Friday Evening.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 27:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 8,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 4) (2)",
        "content": "<p>Blessed be the Lord God, who only does wondrous things! And blessed be His glorious name forever: and let the whole earth be filled with His glory! Daily will I praise the Lord, and at eventime my mouth shall thank Him while I have any being. For when I cry with my voice, He hears me, and gives ear to my supplication. When I pray, He attends to my voice. The Lord is my refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble. Therefore do I laud and magnify You, Eternal God, that You have this day so mercifully kept me from every harm and evil. My heart is glad and my soul glorifies You for Your goodness and mercy. Ever shall my tongue speak of You and say, Blessed be the Lord, and blessed be Your holy name! I ask you, graciously pardon, wherever I have this day sinned against You, and grant me and mine Your protection during the coming night. Be You my shield, and my shade upon my right hand. O Lord, preserve me from all evil, preserve my soul. Be gracious to me, for in You do I put my trust. I trust in the Lord, and cry to God, the Highest, to God Who ends all mine affliction. See, He that keeps Israel shall neither slumber nor sleep. He will guide my steps on the paths of righteousness, that I slip not and my feet do not falter. He will not suffer my feet to be moved, and His word is a light to my path. Therefore as I lay me down, I will not be afraid of sudden terror, neither of the desolation of wicked people, when it comes. For You keep my foot from being taken, and deliverest me from the snares of death. O Lord God, lift upon me the light of Your countenance, that I may lie down and sleep in peace, and dwell in safety under Your protection. For You alone, O Lord, can help me. In Your name will I lie down to rest and let my eyelids slumber. You, O Lord, will again awaken me with rejoicing, to the glory and praise of Your eternal majesty; through Jesus Christ, our Lord. Amen.</p><p>Hinunter ist der Sonnen Schein.</p><p>Sunk is the sun’s last beam of light,</p><p>And now the world is wrapt in night.</p><p>Christ, light us with Your heavenly ray,</p><p>Nor let our feet in darkness stray.</p><p>Thanks, Lord, that You throughout the day</p><p>Have kept all grief and harm away;</p><p>That angels waited round about</p><p>Our coming in and going out.</p><p>Whate’er of wrong we’ve done or said,</p><p>Let not the charge on us be laid;</p><p>That, through Your free forgiveness blest,</p><p>In peaceful slumber we may rest.</p><p>Your guardian angels round us place</p><p>All evil from our couch to chase;</p><p>Our soul and body, while we sleep,</p><p>In safety, gracious Father, keep.</p><p>Nicolaus Hermann, 1560.</p><p>Tr. Frances Elizabeth Cox, 1841.</p><p>Prayer for Saturday Morning.</p><p>O You Very and Eternal God, the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ. To You do I lift up my heart in dutiful gratitude. I will not hide Your righteousness within my heart. I will declare Your salvation. I will not conceal Your loving kindness and Your truth from the great congregation, and all the good that You have shown me will I not keep silent. For it is a good thing to give thanks to the Lord, and to sing praises to Your name, O Most High: to show forth Your loving kindness in the morning, and Your faithfulness every night. Therefore my soul does magnify You, that You in Your immeasurable grace have kept me during the night now past. Blessed be You, Lord God Sabaoth, who are merciful to all, that seek You and love Your salvation. Blessed is Your holy name in all the earth, who are our refuge and our help! Blessed are all Your works which You do for the children of men! I urge you, protect me this day, that the Evil One may not harm me, and the hands of the wicked touch me not. Lord God, my Savior, early will I seek You, early do I cry to You. Grant, that I may fulfill the duties of my calling and all that is committed to me with diligence and trust to the glory of Your name and the betterment of my fellowman, so that I may not misuse the light of this day, neither any of Your creatures in the service of sin and vanity, neither grieve You, nor transgress the covenant of my Baptism with anything I do or leave undone. Vouchsafe to me Your grace, that I may guard myself against the six things which You do hate, yes, seven which are an abomination to You: a proud look, a lying tongue, and hands that shed innocent blood, a heart that devises wicked imaginations, feet that are swift in running to mischief, a false witness that speaks lies, and he that sows discord among brethren. From such and the like vices preserve me, O God, that I may nevermore be led nor consent to them, but teach me to do Your will; for You are my God: Your Spirit is good. Lead me into the land of uprightness, that I may serve You in a life that is without blame, and my deeds and all my conduct be pleasing in Your sight; for Christ’s sake. Amen.</p><p>Die gueldne Sonne.</p><p>The sun, ascending,</p><p>To us is lending</p><p>Bliss, joy, and gladness,</p><p>Cure for all sadness,</p><p>Filling the world with its rich, golden light.</p><p>I was reclining</p><p>When no light was shining;</p><p>But the sun’s beauty</p><p>Now calls me to duty,</p><p>As I see it so fair and so bright.</p><p>Mine eye sees</p><p>What God unfolds:</p><p>Heaven’s bright glory</p><p>Tells me the story</p><p>Of His unlimited power and love,</p><p>And how the sainted</p><p>In beauty untainted,</p><p>Free from things mortal,</p><p>Beyond death’s dark portal,</p><p>Dwell in the heavenly mansions above.</p><p>To God in heaven</p><p>Be praises given;</p><p>Come, let us offer</p><p>And gladly proffer</p><p>To the Creator the gifts that we prize.</p><p>He well receives</p><p>A heart that believes,</p><p>Hymns that adore Him</p><p>Are precious before Him</p><p>And to His throne like sweet incense arise.</p><p>At the day’s ending</p><p>Sweet slumbers sending,</p><p>And in the morning</p><p>All things adorning,</p><p>These are His works and His blessings so true.</p><p>When night descends</p><p>Protection He lends</p><p>When morn appears</p><p>Our spirits He cheers,</p><p>Causing His mercy to crown us anew.</p><p>Father above me,</p><p>You who do love me,</p><p>Bless my beginning,</p><p>Keep me from sinning,</p><p>Move ev’ry hindrance well out of my way.</p><p>Strength ever lend me,</p><p>From Satan defend me,</p><p>Spare me temptation,</p><p>So that in my station</p><p>I may Your holy commandments obey.</p><p>Let me with pleasure</p><p>See the full measure</p><p>Which upon others,</p><p>Who are my brothers,</p>",
        "references": [
          "Proverbs 6:17"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 9,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 5) (1)",
        "content": "<p>You of Your blessings do richly bestow.</p><p>Bid envy vanish!</p><p>All greediness banish!</p><p>Make me Your dwelling,</p><p>Sin’s darkness dispelling.</p><p>Grant that in virtue I daily may grow.</p><p>What is man’s being?</p><p>It is like seeing</p><p>Autumn’s bleak shadows</p><p>Sweep o’er the meadows</p><p>When the cold winds drive the clouds on their way.</p><p>All that we cherish</p><p>Must crumble and perish.</p><p>Plants must stop growing,</p><p>And stars must cease glowing;</p><p>Heaven and earth are not destined to stay.</p><p>All else decays,</p><p>God only stays,</p><p>He of creation</p><p>Is the foundation.</p><p>His will and word must forever abide.</p><p>His grace endures</p><p>And for us secures</p><p>Comfort in sorrow</p><p>And help for the morrow,</p><p>Keeping us cheerful, whate’er may betide.</p><p>God of creation,</p><p>Be my salvation!</p><p>Calm all my terrors,</p><p>Blot out my errors,</p><p>Grant that Your pardon I fully may share;</p><p>Withal attend me,</p><p>Rule, guide, and defend me</p><p>In mercy tender,</p><p>Because I surrender</p><p>Soul, will, and all to Your fatherly care.</p><p>While You are giving</p><p>What for a living</p><p>Seems very needful,</p><p>Oh, make You me heedful</p><p>Of this great truth and commendable thought:</p><p>God, like a tower,</p><p>Transcends all in power;</p><p>Good beyond telling,</p><p>In beauty excelling,</p><p>He does suffice me, all else counts for nothing.</p><p>If grief and sadness</p><p>Temper my gladness</p><p>If for the morrow</p><p>You send me sorrow</p><p>Do as You will, for my trust is in You.</p><p>You surely know</p><p>That what You bestow,</p><p>E’en though distressing,</p><p>Must bring me a blessing;</p><p>You will not deal too severely with me.</p><p>Ills that still grieve me</p><p>Soon are to leave me;</p><p>Though waves may tower</p><p>And winds gain power,</p><p>After the storm the fair sun shows its face.</p><p>Joys e’er increasing,</p><p>And peace never ceasing,</p><p>These I shall treasure</p><p>And share in full measure</p><p>When in His mansions God grants me a place.</p><p>Paul Gerhardt, 1666.</p><p>Tr. H. Brueckner, 1918.</p><p>Prayer for Saturday Evening.</p><p>Praise be to You, You great and unchangeable God! Praise be to Your goodness and mercy! Praise be to Your eternal wisdom and truth, that You have preserved me during the day now past from all danger and harm. I ask you, graciously perfect Your goodness which You have begun in me, and suffer me to rest this night under Your protecting shield, and cover me with Your wings. Suffer me to put my trust under the shadow of Your hands, that I fear no evil. Keep me, O God, as the apple of the eye. Hide me under the shadow of Your wings. Lord, You are the portion of my inheritance; my salvation is in Your hands. Grant to me, according to Your goodness, that neither fear nor trembling come over me, and no terrors of the night overwhelm me. Be merciful to me, for in You do I put my trust, and under the shadow of Your wings do I find my refuge. I seek the Lord in the time of need; my hand is outstretched in the night without ceasing; for my soul has none other comfort; and I know of none other helper in heaven or earth but You alone. At midnight when I awaken, I meditate upon Your name, so altogether lovely, upon Your goodness and fidelity, vouchsafed to me, and I praise You because of Your righteous judgments. When I am troubled I remember God, when my spirit is overwhelmed I speak of my Savior. For He redeems my life from destruction and saves me from the snares of death. Lord God, my Savior, by day and by night do I cry to You, pardon all my transgressions, which during this day and the week now past I have committed against You. O Lord, deliver my soul for Your mercy’s sake. You are gracious and just, and our God is merciful. The Lord preserves the simple. I was brought low, and He helped me. Therefore will I rejoice and praise You, and sing aloud upon my bed. For the days of my life will appear as noonday, and darkness as the morning’s light, and I will rejoice that You, O God, art my hope and my rest in life and death. I lay me down, and none will terrify me. Thus do I commend my body and soul into Your hands, You Faithful God. You have redeemed me through Jesus Christ, our Lord. Amen.</p><p>Werde munter, mein Gemuete.</p><p>Soul of mine, to God awaking,</p><p>And you senses, ev’ry one,</p><p>Come, your quiet haunts forsaking,</p><p>Tell what God to me has done.</p><p>How He this entire day</p><p>Has been with me on my way,</p><p>To my many wants attending</p><p>And from ills protection lending.</p><p>Praise and thanks to You I render,</p><p>Father You of mercies great.</p><p>You have been my strong Defender,</p><p>And Your love does not abate.</p><p>You have shielded me from woe,</p><p>Lent me strength and quenched the foe,</p><p>So that I, such help beholding,</p><p>Rest secure in Your enfolding.</p><p>If from You I have departed,</p><p>I return again to You,</p><p>Knowing You are tender-hearted,</p><p>Since Your Son has died for me.</p><p>I can not deny the guilt,</p><p>But for me His blood was spilt,</p><p>And Your grace, all sin exceeding,</p><p>Lends forgiveness at His pleading.</p><p>O You Light, with brightness filling</p><p>Ev’ry true and pious soul,</p><p>Into me Your grace instilling,</p><p>Make my troubled spirit whole.</p><p>Deign this night to stay with me,</p><p>And let me abide in You,</p><p>That, while darkness may enthrall me,</p><p>Yet no evil may befall me.</p><p>Grant that I in peace may slumber,</p><p>Finding sweet and quiet rest.</p><p>Let no cares my soul encumber,</p><p>Keep it by Your presence blest.</p><p>Mind and body, child and wife,</p><p>All my goods and all my life,</p><p>Friends and foes (again befriended)</p><p>Be this night to You commended.</p><p>Let no terrors overtake me,</p><p>Shield me well from base attack.</p><p>Let no grievous pain awake me,</p><p>War and pestilence keep back.</p><p>Ward off fire, water, death,</p><p>All that threatens life and breath.</p><p>Spare me violence, extortion</p><p>And, withal, a sinner’s portion.</p><p>O immortal God, endue me</p><p>With the gifts for which I ask.</p><p>Jesus, lest some ill pursue me,</p><p>Prosper me in ev’ry task.</p><p>Holy Spirit, comfort, friend,</p><p>On whose counsel I depend,</p><p>Listen to my earnest pleading,</p><p>Amen. You my prayer art heeding.</p><p>Johann Rist, 1642.</p><p>H. Brueckner, 1918.</p><p>A Daily Prayer. To be spoken mornings or evenings.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 17:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 10,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 5) (2)",
        "content": "<p>Dear God and Lord! I live, yet know not how long. I die, yet know not when. You, O Heavenly Father, know. Look, dear Lord, is this hour, this day (this night) the last of my life: Your will be done! You alone know best. As You will I am willing through the true faith in Jesus Christ, my Redeemer, to live or die. But, O God, do You grant me this petition, that I may not suddenly pass away in my sins, and be lost. Vouchsafe to me true knowledge, repentance and sorrow over my passed transgressions. Set them before my sight in this life, that they may not at the last day be set before me, and I be put to shame before angels and men. Grant me sufficient time and opportunity for repentance, so that from all my heart I may know and acknowledge my transgressions, and from Your saving word obtain forgiveness and comfort for the same. O Merciful Father, forsake me not, and take not Your Holy Spirit from me. My heart and my heart’s trust, O You Searcher of hearts, is ever known to You. Keep me in such trust to the life eternal. May I die, when You will, only grant me a peaceful and blessed End. Amen.</p><p>Lord Jesus Christ, You highest good,</p><p>I ask you, through Your precious blood,</p><p>Grant, that my final end be good. Amen.</p><p>At the Beginning of the Week’s Work.</p><p>Rule You, O God the Father, who has made us, and like all other creatures ordained us not to indolence but to work, and bless each one in his calling. You who rulest the universe also rule our own dear government and graciously vouchsafe to it Your wisdom and strength.</p><p>Rule You, O God the Son, who has redeemed and ransomed us from sin. Take from us the burden of sin committed during the week now past, and graciously grant us Your peace. You the Supreme Bishop and Archshepherd of our souls, help all servants of Your word in this and all Your congregations on earth to labor and bring forth much fruit to eternal life.</p><p>Rule You, O God the Holy Spirit, who has sanctified us and born us again in Holy Baptism. Create in us a clean heart and renew a right spirit within us, that we carry no evils of the past into the new week, but put away all purpose and inclination of the old Adam still in us. Govern You our hearts with power; and if this week mark for any of us the end of life, help You in the last bitter hour. Fill the heart with that grace which is better than life. Teach the hands to battle and vanquish the last enemy, and grant for Christ’s sake, the rest and triumph of the sabbath everlasting.</p><p>You, the Triune and Immortal God, be and abide with us and Your Church forever. To You be glory, laud, and honor, world without end. Amen.</p><p>At the Table</p><p>Grace Before Food.</p><p>The eyes of all wait upon You, O God, and You give them their food in due season. You open Your hand and satisfiest the desire of every living thing. Amen.</p><p>O Lord God, Heavenly Father, bless us and these Your gifts, which we receive from Your loving kindness, through Jesus Christ, our Lord. Amen.</p><p>Come Lord Jesus, be our guest,</p><p>And let Your gifts to us be blest.</p><p>Amen.</p><p>Gracious Father, who feed and nourishest every living creature, feed and nourish our souls and bodies that we may not abuse Your gifts, but that we be rather quickened by the same to the glory of Your Name, to all honest toil and every good work, to live and move before You in righteousness and innocence. Amen.</p><p>1562.</p><p>Be present at our table Lord;</p><p>Be here and everywhere adored.</p><p>Your people bless, and grant that we,</p><p>May dwell in paradise with You.</p><p>Amen.</p><p>Heavenly Father, bless this food,</p><p>To Your glory and our good.</p><p>Amen.</p><p>Jesus, bless what You have given,</p><p>Feed our souls with bread from Heaven;</p><p>Guide and lead us all the way,</p><p>In all that we may do and say.</p><p>Amen.</p><p>Grace After Food.</p><p>O give thanks to the Lord, for He is good; for His mercy endures forever. He gives food to all flesh; He gives to the beast his food; and to the young ravens which cry. The Lord takes pleasure in them that fear Him, in those that hope in His mercy. Amen.</p><p>We thank You, Lord God, Heavenly Father, through Jesus Christ, our Lord, for all Your benefits; who live and reign forever and ever. Amen.</p><p>Dearest God, for food and drink</p><p>Accept our praise. Your Name be blessed.</p><p>Amen.</p><p>Bless the Lord, O my soul, and all that is within me, bless His holy Name. Bless the Lord, O my soul, and forget not all His benefits. Amen.</p><p>We thank You, Lord, for this our food,</p><p>We thank You more for Jesus’ blood,</p><p>Let manna to our souls be given,</p><p>The bread of life sent down from heaven.</p><p>Amen.</p><p>Praise God from whom all blessings flow,</p><p>Praise Him all creatures here below;</p><p>Praise Him above you heavenly host;</p><p>Praise Father, Son, and Holy Spirit.</p><p>Amen.</p><p>For Hearth and Home</p><p>Prayer of a Housefather.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 103:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 11,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 6) (1)",
        "content": "<p>Gracious and Eternal God! Who Yourself in Paradise have ordained holy matrimony, vouchsafe to me Your grace and help in my married life, that I may ever maintain the true Christian love and fidelity toward my spouse. Grant, that I may dwell with her, as with the weaker vessel, in kindness and harmony according to knowledge, giving honor to her, as being heirs together of the grace of life, and guide her together with my children and servants to the true knowledge of Your divine glory, and to modesty and honesty. Grant to them also, O Lord, that they follow You, and suffer themselves to be guided. Preserve us, O You God of Peace, from strife and dissensions, and the calumnies of the enemy, from undue suspicion, which the devil sows as a seed of perdition, and for the destruction of conjugal love and faith. Grant, that as Your children we may suffer and forgive each other in love and charity. Give us faithful servants and keep them in good health. Bless our labor and all that contributes to the wants of the body. Enable us to bear our cross with patience, and grant that we be together again in the after-life. Amen.</p><p>Prayer of a Housemother.</p><p>O God Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, You giver of all blessing and consolation, see me, whom You have placed in the estate of holy wedlock and made me the mother of this family, with the eyes of Your mercy. Vouchsafe to Your handmaiden Your grace, that I may love You above all things, seek You, and ever be diligent in Your service. And grant that next to You I may honor, fear, and love my husband, and obey him with patience and kindness, in pure and modest conduct, in piety and humility well pleasing to You, and that the hidden life of my heart be constantly adorned with a meek and gentle disposition and every virtue, even as in former times the consecrated women, who trusted in God and continued in subjection to their husbands. Enable me to train my children and servants with meekness, to the honor and glory of Your holy name. Give grace, that they follow me with gentleness, and grant, that I with my husband and family may satisfy the wants of this life in good health and according to Your divine will. Protect us from harm and from enemies. Enable us so to use this world that we be not hindered in our salvation, but in all things seek You, O Lord, and endeavor to be well-favored in Your sight. May we not despise the cross neither murmur against it, but bear it in patience, and thus remain in Your keeping to the end. May we as servants of God bring forth abundant fruitage, live holy lives in this world, and attain to the everlasting inheritance in the world to come. Amen.</p><p>Prayer of a Child.</p><p>Almighty and Eternal God! You have commanded me to honor my parents, and in all things, which are not contrary to Your word, to obey them. I urge you, for the sake of the obedience of Your dear Son, Jesus Christ, my Lord, grant to me, that I may duly honor my father and mother, serve them, obey, love, and esteem them, so that their blessing may dwell with me to the end of my days. Keep me from sin and evil communications, that I may not anger or grieve my dear parents with hatred, sadness, scornfulness, disobedience, or obstinacy, and thus bring upon myself on this earth already their and Your curse, and in the after-life Your eternal wrath. And since I have sinned many times through weakness, I urge you, work in me sorrow and true repentance, through Jesus Christ, my Savior, who gave to me the example of true obedience. Grant, that according to His example I may ever grow, and increase in wisdom, stature, and grace before You, O God, and all men. Amen.</p><p>Prayer of a Youth or a Maiden.</p><p>Lord, Almighty and Holy God, who love a pure heart, a chaste mind, and a holy life: I ask you, create in me also a clean heart, and renew a right spirit within me, so that I may serve You with sincere faith and true fear, and love You with all my strength. And keep me from all impure communications. Subdue the evil lusts in my heart, and extinguish the fires of fleshly desire. Preserve me from unchaste and dissolute associations, from rioting and drunkenness, which lead to excess. May all filthiness, and foolish talking, unbecoming a Christian, be ever foreign to my life. Keep me from arrogant pomp, or idleness and lounging, from the snares and nets of the devil. Grant, that I may serve You with a pure soul and an undefiled body in true faith, as did pious and chaste Joseph in Egypt, and graciously take me into Your safekeeping against all temptations and seductions of life, for the sake of Jesus Christ, Your dear Son. Amen.</p><p>Prayer of a Servant.</p>",
        "references": [
          "1 Peter 3:7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 12,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 6) (2)",
        "content": "<p>Merciful God, Who through the precious suffering and death of Your dear Son, Jesus Christ, have redeemed and made me free from eternal servitude, from the power of sin, from the dominion of darkness, from the cruel tyranny of the devil, and the mastery of death and hell: I urge you, grant me grace, so that in my present station and calling, in which You have placed me on earth according to Your good pleasure, I may not be dissatisfied, nor murmur impatiently against Your ordinance, nor yet envy others in their more exalted stations, but help me to do such Your will from the heart, with good will, ever thinking that I serve You, O God in Heaven, and not men. Help You, dear Father, that I serve You, the supreme Lord of heaven and earth, in true knowledge and upright fear, love You above all things, put all my hope and salvation in You, and continue in Your commandments without blame. May I also be subject to my masters and mistresses according to the flesh, not only to the good and gentle, but also to the froward, and patiently obey them in everything that is not contrary to Your pleasure, with fear and singleness of heart, as to Christ my Lord, not with eye-service, as man pleaser, but from the depths of my heart and for the sake of Your will and commandment. Grant me grace that I may be found faithful in all things committed and entrusted to my care, not neglect nor bring them to nothing, suffer no harm to come through carelessness on my part; also that I may not covet nor fraudulently appropriate to my own use the possessions of another entrusted to my care. Preserve my health, strengthen my limbs and all the powers of my body. Endow me with wisdom and understanding, that I may perform the labor of my masters and mistresses, with Your help improve their living. May they be blessed through my industry; and all that I do and perform, may it redound to Your divine glory and a blessed exercise of my faith. For Christ, Your dear Son’s sake, who lives and reigns with You and the Holy Spirit, world without end. Amen.</p><p>Prayer of a School Child for the Holy Spirit.</p><p>O my dear Lord, Jesus Christ, I thank You, that to the present day, You ordain church and school ordinances and regulations, and have given to my parents and me grace, that I too may be thus trained. I urge you, fill me with Your Holy Spirit, that I may ever obey my dear parents and teachers, who only seek my welfare. Give to me a docile heart, that I may learn my catechism, noble arts and language, and thus increase in godliness, wisdom, understanding, and every virtue. O my dear Lord Jesus Christ, create in me a pure, chaste, and modest heart. May I ever serve You in upright faith and true fear, and love You from all my heart. Subdue in me all evil lusts. Endow me with Your Holy Spirit. Help me to continue in true humility. Grant me an obedient heart, to honor my parents according to Your commandment, and neither anger nor grieve them. May they live long on this earth, and protect You and preserve them from disease, evil, and harm. Be gracious to us and merciful. Bless us in body and soul, now and forevermore. Amen.</p><p>Prayer During a Thunder Storm.</p><p>Most Mighty God! All the powers of the earth shall honor Your holy name, and worship You, Eternal Father, in the beauty of holiness. For You are the Lord, who reigns over all. You show Your might and power throughout the universe. The voice of the Lord is upon the waters. The Lord of glory thunders. The Lord is upon many waters. The voice of the Lord is powerful and full of majesty. The earth shook and trembled. The foundations also of the hills moved and were shaken. There went up smoke out of Your nostrils, and fire out of Your mouth devoured: coals are kindled by it. Your pavilion round about You are dark waters and thick clouds of the skies. The Lord thunders in the heavens, and the Highest gave His voice. All things are subject to You. All things acknowledge You as their maker, and tremble before Your divine majesty. The mountains and the depths of the abyss are sore vexed when You are wroth, the earth trembles, and the sea and the waters flee because of Your wrath. The voice of the Lord divides the flames of fire. The voice of the Lord shakes the wilderness. The Lord is king forever. The Lord will give strength to His people. The Lord will bless His people with peace. O merciful God, preserve us from Your wrath, which is beyond endurance! Forgive us our sins! Make Your face shine upon us! Cause this grievous tempest to pass by without harm to us! Protect us in body and soul, our house and home. Keep the fruits of the fields from hail and storm, from great inundation by water and all harm. O Holy God, preserve us from an evil death, and protect us, that no disaster befall us. Amen.</p><p>Thanksgiving After a Thunderstorm.</p><p>Almighty God, our Heavenly Father, who has said, Call upon Me in the day of trouble: I will deliver you, and you will glorify Me; we praise and thank You most heartily. For You have graciously heard our prayer and have made this storm to pass that no harm befell us in life and property. Thus Have you again revealed to us Your fatherly compassion, and that You would not deal with us after our sins, neither reward us according to our iniquities. Grant us, O Merciful Father, for the sake of Your only begotten Son, Jesus Christ, that we may take heed of Your earnest and fatherly admonitions, improve our lives, and live in the fear of You. May we constantly prepare and make ready ourselves for the coming of Your dear Son, when the elements shall melt with fervent heat, and the earth also, and the works of it, shall be burned up, and may we meet Him with rejoicing and enter the new heaven, in which dwells righteousness, and forever be with You; through the same, Your dear Son, our Master and Redeemer, Jesus Christ. Amen.</p><p>Prayer on the Eve of a Journey.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 18:7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 13,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 6) (3)",
        "content": "<p>Almighty and Gracious God and Father, You protector of all that trust in You from their hearts! In Your name will I proceed and undertake this journey. For You are my God and preserve my going out and my coming in. You lead my feet in right paths and will not suffer them to be moved. I heartily Urge you to be my gracious guide and companion on this present journey. Send Your holy angels, and command them, in all my wanderings, to keep me from all evil in body and soul. Lead me on the paths of the righteous and bring me safely to my destination, that I may laud and magnify You, here in time and in eternity forever. And now, O Lord God and Father, into Your hands do I commit my body and soul and all that I possess. Your holy angel be my safe guard. Amen.</p><p>Prayer During a Journey.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Timothy 4:6-8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 14,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 7) (1)",
        "content": "<p>Almighty and Most Merciful God! We are always in Your sight wherever we be. You preserve our coming in and our going out, and lead us on the right paths that we slip not. I ask you, that as You did lead Your servant Abraham from the land of the Chaldees and kept him unharmed in his pilgrimage, and did say to his grandson Jacob when he journeyed to Mesopotamia, I am with you, and will bring you again into this land; and as You also did lead the Children of Israel through the Red Sea and through the desert, and did go before them, by day in a pillar of a cloud and night in a pillar of fire: thus Would you also be with me on my wandering, protect me on land and sea, by day and by night, and keep me from all harm and danger. And when my business is completed bring me home again in full health of body and soul. And as You did accompany youthful Tobias through Your angel Raphael, likewise do You accompany me in all my ways, so that when I, too, have happily returned to my home, I with all my own may have the greater reason to laud and praise You as my true and faithful guide. Meanwhile I commend to Your care all that I leave at home, and Urge you to have charge concerning them, and suffer me to find them unharmed when I return. Amen.</p><p>Prayer of Children for Their Father Engaged on a Journey.</p><p>O Eternal Son of God, You Savior of all who call upon You in faith! We Your children, baptized into Your blood, consecrated by Your Spirit a royal priesthood, and ordained Your brethren and co-heirs with You in grace, call upon You with innocent tongues, and earnestly Ask you, graciously to safeguard our dear father now journeying over land for the sake of his calling and to gain the means of livelihood. Help him to discharge his duties with favor and despatch, and return him to us in health and joy, as You through Your angels did preserve and guide Your servant Jacob on his journeys, for You are the faithful guide and companion of all who fear You and trust in Your mercy. Amen.</p><p>Thanksgiving After a Completed Journey.</p><p>Gracious God and Father! Most heartily do I thank You that You have enabled me to bring my journey to a happy end. Through the ministrations of Your dear angels You have again brought me to my home, guarded and kept me from all evil, preserved me from the murderous and robbing hands of evildoers, and the teeth of wild beasts, and kept me from all other dangers of body and soul. In short, that I have been led to and fro in health and happiness: I owe it altogether to Your fatherly goodness and almighty care. And I urge you from all my heart, continue to keep me and mine under Your protection, and preserve us, body and soul, to the eternal life, for Jesus’ sake. Amen.</p><p>A Birthday Prayer.</p><p>Dear Father in heaven, I thank You from all my heart, that You have put me into this world and made me a rational being. I am born of Christian parents and made a member of Your holy Church. Today the anniversary of my birth has come, and since I have been permitted to reach this day and thus complete another year of my pilgrimage, I thank You from all my heart and joyfully reiterate the thanksgiving of Your servant David, “Bless the Lord, O my soul, and all that is within me bless His holy name. Bless the Lord, O my soul, and forget not all His benefits: who forgives all your iniquities; who heals all your diseases; who redeems your life from destruction; who crowns you with loving kindness and tender mercies.”</p><p>Since every day of my life, however, is one step nearer to death, which can strike me this hour, yes, this very minute, I urge you so to shape and rule every day of my life, that I may walk according to Your pleasure as in the day, that is circumspectly in Your sight, honestly, and conscious of my responsibility, in short as a true Christian and in conformity with the promise made by me to You, my dear God, in my baptism. And if this prove my last year and my last birthday, I place all things into Your gracious keeping. If it is Your will that I should cease to live, then I have lived enough. For if it is sufficient for You, it is sufficient for me. Am I old enough for You, I am old enough for me. Here I again put myself under Your shield and protection, into Your sublime and eternal power. If I live this year, may I live in You; if I die, may I die in You, so that I may live, and move, and have my being in You, and whether living or dying I may be Your to all eternity. Amen.</p><p>Prayer for Temporal Peace.</p><p>Eternal God, Everlasting Father! You are a God and lover of peace. From You all true unity comes. We ask you graciously to protect Your Christendom on earth against all its enemies, so that we may be kept in peace, and ever serve You gladly in faithful doctrine and a pure conduct. Grant us grace, so that all estates and rulers of Christendom may live peacefully and harmoniously in perfect piety and godliness, so that discipline and order prevail, churches and schools be not destroyed, and the country be not devastated nor grievously oppressed. Grant us grace, so that men will content themselves with what they have, and will not for the sake of avarice or lusting after foreign lands and peoples, nor yet because of pride, vain ambitions, and arrogance, enmity, hatred, envy, nor any other cause, incite war, sedition, or revolution in this our country. Hinder all evil counsel and purpose of unstable men, who think only of that which is not good. Put them to nothing in their purposes, so that they must retreat and are utterly consumed with terrors. Stretch forth Your arm to protect us who are named after You, so that Your heritage be not destroyed. Support Your faithful who rely upon You and call upon Your name. Hear us in our distress, and Your holy name protect us. Send us help from Your sanctuary, and strengthen us from on high. Bless the country and the cities in which Your holy word dwells. Prosperity must dwell within their palaces! O merciful God, incline the hearts of all men to a Christian peace and concord, to the which You have called us through Your word and gospel. And if bitterness prevail among some, help that it be done away with, to the glory of Your holy name, the spreading of Your word, and the betterment of Christendom, and that the poor and distressed in the land may rejoice in You and praise Your holy name, for You only performest wonders and provest Your powers among the nations. Amen.</p><p>A Prayer for School.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 103:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 15,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 7) (2)",
        "content": "<p>We ask you, everlasting God, Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, You eternal and inseparable Trinity and inexpressible Unity, that You would faithfully take under Your protecting wing the flock of Your Christendom, and ever abide in our midst with Your grace and truth. Be You with us, O Lord, our God. Be You a wall of fire round about us, and destroy them who hate You and are hostile to Your name. So rule us, O God, that we may ever be guided by Your clear and pure word and are not seduced by the external appearance of things. Keep us, Lord Jesus, from error and false doctrine, and send us faithful teachers who take heed to Your congregation, purchased with Your blood, and are anxious to perform Your will. Grant us obedient hearts, so that we, as lambs of Your flock, may obey Your voice, and be filled with fruits of righteousness. Teach us ever to do Your will, for You are our God: Your spirit is good. Lead us into the land of uprightness, to the end that we, too, through a blessed departure from this life may attain to You and the everlasting joy and blessedness, and see Your glory to all eternity. Amen.</p><p>Church Prayers</p><p>Prayer When Going to Church.</p><p>Almighty God, Heavenly Father, because of Your great mercy I will go to Your house and worship You in Your temple in Your fear. Lord, lead me in the paths of righteousness, and make Your way straight before my face. Guide me on the paths of Your commandments, for You are my God, and the Lord of my salvation. I delight in Your sanctuary and rejoice in the congregation of Your saints, who confess and glorify You. How amiable are Your tabernacles, O Lord of hosts! My soul longs, yes, even faints for the courts of the Lord. O come, let us worship and bow down: let us kneel before the Lord our maker. For He is our God; and we are the people of His pasture, and the sheep of His hand. Magnify the Lord our God, adore at His footstool; for He is holy. I worship You, O God, in the accepted time through Your great mercy. Hear me according to Your grace. Amen.</p><p>Prayer for Sincere Repentance.</p><p>Merciful and Gracious God, You are slow to anger and plenteous in mercy! You call us daily through the gracious preaching of Your word to devout conversion, and in Your name causest repentance and remission of sins to be preached. And You show Your forbearance with us through Your long suffering and inexpressible mercy, and do not suddenly come upon sinners in the midst of their evil deeds with Your righteous wrath and judgment to punish them, but gives place and time for repentance, so that no one can justly charge or accuse You. For You are not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance and have everlasting life. O dear God, You know the sluggishness of our flesh and the hardness of our hearts, that we through inherited sin are thus far deranged and so deeply sunk in sin, that of our own accord we can not rise or return. Therefore, for the sake of the wounds of Jesus Christ, my Lord, I urge you, convert You me, and I am converted. For You are my God, and where I am converted I will truly repent. Save You me, O Lord, and I am saved. Help me, and I am helped. See, I am like an erring and lost sheep. Seek You Your servant, that I forget not Your commandments. Circumcise the foreskin of my heart. Purge me, and I shall be clean. Wash me, and I shall be whiter than snow. Create in me a clean heart, O God; and renew a right spirit within me. Cast me not away from Your presence; and take not Your Holy Spirit from me. O dear God, look upon me, as You did look upon Mary Magdalene, the repentant sinner, as she lay at Your feet and wept over her transgressions; and the publican in the temple, as he smote his breast and besought Your grace. Vouchsafe to me sincere sorrow and contrition over my sin, and a true faith with firm confidence in Your grace, and also worthy fruits of repentance. Let me discern the day of Your visitation, and not despise the riches of Your mercy, so that I may not neglect the accepted time, and the day of Your salvation, and not fail to turn to You, my Lord and God. May I not postpone my repentance from one day to another, nor yet to the last hour, but rather turn to You this day and repent. Amen.</p><p>Prayer for the Forgiveness of Sins.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 95:7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 16,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 8) (1)",
        "content": "<p>Merciful Father, Eternal God, my sins are grievous, many and great my transgressions, and mine iniquities are innumerable, for the imaginations of my heart are evil from my youth. O Lord, who can understand his errors? See, I acknowledge my transgressions: my sin is ever before me. Against You only have I sinned, and done evil in Your sight: that You might be justified when You speak, and be clear when You judge. I urge Your infinite mercy, enter not into judgment with Your servant: for in Your sight shall no living man be justified. If You, Lord, should mark iniquities, O Lord, who shall stand? See, if You contend with man, he can not answer You one of a thousand, for all our righteousness before You is as the filthy rag. Have mercy upon me, O God, according to Your loving kindness: according to the multitude of Your tender mercies, blot out my transgressions. Wash me thoroughly from mine iniquity, and cleanse me from my sin for Your name’s sake. Lord, have mercy upon me, save my soul, for, alas! I have sinned against You. Remember, O Lord, Your tender mercies and Your loving kindnesses; for they have been ever as of old. Remember not the sins of my youth, nor my transgressions: according to Your mercy remember You me for Your goodness’ sake, O Lord. Remember that we are flesh, as the wind which blows and does not return, and cease in Your anger and wrath against us. O merciful God, I acknowledge that my virtues and my deeds can never blot out my sins, nor yet merit Your grace. Only the innocent suffering and death of Jesus Christ, the Lamb without spot or blemish, is the true offering for our iniquities, and His blood, shed for the remission of our sins, is the cleansing and purification of our souls. In such confidence and hope I supplicate You: forgive You the transgressions of Your people. Cover our sins. Impute not our iniquities, for You are merciful. Cleanse You me from secret fault. Let my sorrowing soul and my vexed bones again rejoice, for with You there is mercy and plenteous redemption. O Lord, hear the voice of my supplication, and despise not the groanings of my heart, for Christ’s sake. Amen.</p><p>Prayer for True Faith.</p><p>Lord, Almighty God, You Father of lights, with whom there is no variableness, neither shadow of turning, from whom every good and perfect gift comes, I ask you, since all men have not faith: implant and maintain in my heart through the workings of Your Holy Spirit the true knowledge of Your dear Son Jesus Christ, and increase it from day to day, so that I, too, may be filled with the knowledge of Your will in all wisdom and spiritual understanding, that I may walk worthy of You to all pleasing, being faithful in every good work, and increase in such knowledge according to Your glorious power in all patience and long suffering with joy. Grant to me, according to the riches of Your glory, that I may be strengthened with might by Your Spirit in the inner man, that Christ may dwell in my heart by faith. O dear God, since no man knows the Son but You, O Father, and no man knows You, the Father, but only the same Your Son, and he to whom the Son will reveal You, I ask you, draw You me to You. Grant me the knowledge of salvation, which is the forgiveness of sins. Strengthen my weak faith, which is small as the mustard seed, so that it may increase, and I be rooted and grounded in You, and may stand steadfast and unmoveable. Gracious God, You have kindled the spark of faith in my heart and has begun this good work in me, I cry to You, perfect it until the end, that we may ever increase in knowledge and understanding, and be sincere and without offense till the day of Christ, being filled with the fruits of righteousness, which are by Jesus Christ, to the glory and praise of God. Preserve what You have begun, that we might war a good warfare, holding faith and a good conscience, and not waver or succumb in trial and temptation and make shipwreck concerning faith. Therefore protect me, my God, that I am not led astray among the errors, schisms, and heresies of the world. Preserve me from superstitions and all false doctrine, that I may neither err nor doubt in any article of faith. And vouchsafe to me Your grace, that my faith be not lifeless, inactive, or without good fruits, but active and energetic, serving in love, so that I, too, may carry off the end of faith, which is the soul’s salvation. Amen.</p><p>Prayer Before Confession.</p><p>Lord Jesus Christ, You my Redeemer, You have given to Your dear Church on earth and its faithful servants the sacred office of the keys, and invested it with the promise, that whatever by virtue of the same they shall loose or bind on earth shall also be loosed or bound in heaven: I thank You, and eternally praise and glorify You, for such Your gracious gift. And, since I a poor and bound sinner need this blessed key which loosens, so that I may not be kept under the bonds of the infernal jailer, I urge Your infinite mercy, that I may receive its comfort through my spiritual father, my pastor, and for the sake of Your holy, precious blood and Your innocent offering and death be loosed from all my sins. Grant me Your Holy Spirit, so that I may grasp this holy absolution in heartfelt contrition and undoubting trust, firm resolve, brotherly love and gratitude, and inherit the eternal life. Amen.</p><p>Thanksgiving After Absolution.</p><p>O Blessed, Merciful, and Gracious God! I thank and praise You from all my heart, that through Your servant You have again forgiven me, a poor sinner, all my sins, again received me in grace, and promised me eternal life. I earnestly Ask you, vouchsafe to me Your Holy Spirit, and create in me a pure heart, so that I may joyously trust that all my sins are forgiven me through Jesus Christ. As an earnest and surety of this blessed fact, I will now eat and drink the true body and blood of Your dear Son Jesus Christ, under the bread and wine, to my soul’s salvation. Grant me also, O faithful God, that from now on I may earnestly avoid all sin and better my life. This You would graciously grant me for Your name’s sake. Amen.</p><p>A Morning Prayer for Communion Day.</p><p>Arise, my soul, this is the day which the Lord has made. We will rejoice and be glad in it. Give thanks to the gracious and merciful God for His blessings and say:</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 25:7"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 17,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 8) (2)",
        "content": "<p>Almighty and Merciful God and Father, I thank You from all my heart for the protection of this night, for the refreshing rest, and for the joyous morning, which You have granted to me. I praise You with all my soul for Your wonderful mercy which blesses me with the forgiveness of my sins. Praised be Your grace, which is new each morn, and which on this day also bids me to Your house, and calls and invites me to Your altar.</p><p>O Lord, since I, too, would come to Your Supper with the throngs which celebrate, do You Yourself make me ready. As You would find a pure residence in me, do You cleanse and consecrate my body and soul. Guide me with Your eye, and lead me with Your hand to the riches of Your mercy. Comfort me with Your countenance, and do not forsake me. As the hart pants after the water brooks, so pants my soul after You, O God.</p><p>And, that my sacred resolve may not be hampered, I commend to You my body and soul, reason, senses, and thoughts, whatever I do or leave undone, my coming in and going out, my walking, standing, sitting, and resting, my imaginations and aspirations, my faith and confession, and whatever internally or externally I may be or do. O God, preserve in me a devout spirit, and hinder whatever might disturb or hamper me. Receive me into the especial care of Your grace, and increase in me the work that is now begun. Perfect and complete it according to Your power and grace to Your glory and my salvation. Keep me from evil thoughts, from idle imaginations, from all uncleanliness, so that in Your fear I may begin a consecrated life and continue in it. May the light of my faith shine before men. May I give offense to none, but rather in Christian conduct edify the brethren and direct them to all virtue.</p><p>Holy Jesus, do You unite with my body and soul on this day. Nourish me with Your flesh and refresh me with Your blood, so that my weak faith be strengthened, and receive the assurance of Your grace, the remission of my sin, and eternal salvation. Invest me with the pure silk of Your righteousness. Clothe me in the true wedding garment, that I may appear at Your heavenly board a worthy guest.</p><p>Now, Lord God and Father, be my help and my protection! Lord Jesus Christ be my bread, my light, and life! And You, O Holy Spirit, illumine and preserve me in the true sanctification, so that in that estate, into which I again am permitted to enter, I may remain for the course of my life. Let me be enveloped in You. Without You there is only grief. O dear Savior, let me ever be with You. Amen.</p><p>Prayer Before Holy Communion.</p><p>Lord Jesus Christ, Eternal Son of God, I am not worthy to open my lips and receive the most precious sacrament of Your body and blood. For I am a sin-stained man, but You are the Lord whom the heavens can not encompass. How then can a human being who is but dust and ashes be worthy to receive Your most holy body and precious blood! I well know and acknowledge that my sins are many and that for that reason I am an unworthy guest at Your table. But I also sincerely believe and confess it with my lips that by Your grace You can render me worthy. For You are omnipotent and gracious. You only can cleanse and make holy whatever took rise in unclean seed. You can transform sinners into true and holy men, when by Your grace You forgive sin and renew us with Your Holy Spirit. Therefore I ask you, by Your power and love grant me grace, that I may worthily approach Your altar, and not become guilty of Your body and blood by unworthy eating and drinking, so that I may not receive death in place of life. Grant me grace, that I may know and test myself as a poor sinner, my heart filled with sorrow over mine iniquity, and may properly discern Your tender and noble body, and Your holy, precious blood. May my reason, senses, and intellect be ever submissive to Your word, and may I be earnestly resolved to better and improve my life with Your help, so that in this precious sacrament, I may not only with my mouth receive Your body and blood, but also in true faith accept You, my Savior and Redeemer, enthrone You in my heart, and find in You my life and blessedness. For You are the living bread which comes down from heaven and brings life to men. Whoever comes to You shall nevermore hunger, and he that believes on You shall nevermore thirst. Whoever eats Your flesh and drinks Your blood dwells in You and You in him, and shall never die. O beloved Lord, my spirit and my mind yearn for You. As the hart pants after the water brooks, so pants my soul after You, O God. My soul thirsts for God, for the living God: when shall I come and appear before God? Fill me with Your grace. Amen.</p><p>A Sigh When About to Receive the Sacred Body of Christ.</p><p>Lord Jesus Christ, Your holy body strengthen and preserve me in the true faith to eternal life. Amen.</p><p>A Sigh When About to Receive the Sacred Blood of Christ.</p><p>Lord Jesus Christ, Your holy blood strengthen and preserve me in the true faith to eternal life. Amen.</p><p>Prayer After the Holy Supper.</p><p>Lord Jesus Christ, with all my heart I thank and glorify You, that You have again cleansed me, a poor sinner, from all my sin, and as an earnest of such cleansing and forgiveness of my sin, have nourished me with Your body and blood, and like an unclean child, after such purification, have received me into the fatherly arms of Your grace and mercy, and put me pure, reproachless, and without blemish before Your Father.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalms 42:2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 18,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 9) (1)",
        "content": "<p>I earnestly Ask you with all my power, in addition to such blessing, grant me Your grace through the workings of Your Holy Spirit, so that I may sufficiently understand such blessing and grace, gratefully accept it, and glorify and praise You with all my heart. Grant me strength from above by Your Holy Spirit, that I may heartily forgive my neighbor wherever he has sinned against me, even as You have fully and richly forgiven me my great and manifold transgressions, yes, entirely blotted them out and will never remember them. Help me to love my neighbor and gladly show him every good, as You have done to me, and have shown me more than I can ever sufficiently thank You for. Praise and glory be to You, O faithful God, together with the Father and the Holy Spirit, world without end. Amen.</p><p>Prayer for a Pious Life.</p><p>O my dear Lord Jesus, illumine me today and forever, that I may shape the course of my Christian life and direct it toward the eternal Jerusalem, my eternal home. And as You yearn for me, may I also have all my delight and thirst in You, seek You early, yearn for You, and make of You, the bread of life, the companion of all my ways. Keep me, O unchangeable, everlasting God, from the inconstancy of the children of this world, that I may not fall into hypocrisy as they do, but today and always, in all my calling, prove myself constant in godliness, so that my life may decrease in vice and increase in virtue. May I always faithfully serve You, my Lord, disdain the worldly, be exalted in You, experience Your grace and protection, and eternally thank You, for Christ’s sake. Amen.</p><p>Prayer for Faithful Teachers and Preachers.</p><p>The harvest truly is plenteous, but the laborers are few; pray you, therefore, the Lord of the harvest, that He will send forth laborers into His harvest.</p><p>Merciful God, who has commanded us through Your only begotten Son to Ask you for laborers in Your harvest: I earnestly Urge you, grant us, Your sheep, pastors according to Your heart, to feed us with doctrine and wisdom. Put learned bishops, Christian pastors, pious teachers over Your congregation, faithfully to show the true way to eternal life. Fill them plenteously with Your Holy Spirit, so that they may fruitfully proclaim the holy and precious word of the gospel, and sincerely perfect Your work. Grant them a courageous heart, that without fear or favor they may lift up their voices and put the adversaries to silence. Open the door for their word, that they may be blessed and go from strength to strength. May they also be shining examples for the flocks entrusted to their care. Grant me and all listeners a grateful heart, that we may communicate in all good things to them that teach us the word, rejoice in them, forsake them not, so that we may be blessed of You according to Your promise in all the works of our hands. Preserve us from hirelings, time-servers, unfaithful laborers, and hypocrites, who falsely deceive the hearts of the righteous, whom You have never grieved, and fortify the hands of the wicked, that they will not turn from their evil ways. Let us never be robbed of our faithful preachers for the sake of our ingratitude, but rather look upon the glory of Your holy name, yes, see the Kingdom of Your Anointed, that it may prevail among us to the end of the world. Amen.</p><p>Prayer for the Kingdom of God. (Meeting of the Congregation)</p><p>Gracious and Blessed God, who has taught, and commanded us above all things and first to seek the kingdom of God and His righteousness: I ask you, grant us grace, that Your holy word may be preached in all the world in all its truth and purity, and we submit our reason to the obedience of faith, and live holy lives according to it as behooves the children of God to Your pleasing, so that Your kingdom may come to us, and increase, and many of them, who do not yet believe in the word, be won through a Christian conduct. Help us, dear God, who are delivered from the power of darkness and are translated into the kingdom of Your dear Son Jesus Christ, in whom we have redemption through His blood, even the forgiveness of sin, that we may remain in His kingdom, faithfully continue in the wholesome doctrine, and live worthily as children of light in all piety and godliness. And since the kingdom of God comes not with outward show, neither consists of mere words, but is power and spirit: grant us grace, that we may be born again from above through Your saving word and Your Holy Spirit, co-heirs of life, so that with our hearts we may dwell above where Christ sits, and constantly seek the inheritance incorruptible, and undefiled, that fades not away. Enable us to be poor in the spirit and humble, and such who sorrow over their sins. Let us be anhungered and thirsty, and heartily yearn after righteousness. May we ever be meek, and suffer and overcome whatever of persecution and tribulation may assail us, and revilings and undeserved malignings with patience and longsuffering. Keep us from all offenses, by which Your holy name is blasphemed and outraged, Your kingdom hindered and weakened. Grant us grace to practice our faith in works of love and mercy, feeding, clothing, harboring, visiting, comforting the poor and distressed of this world, so that when that great day dawns we may hear the blessed and joyous voice of Your dear Son: Come you blessed of My Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world. Amen.</p><p>Prayer for Missions.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 25:34"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 19,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 9) (2)",
        "content": "<p>I ask you, my heavenly Father, through Jesus Christ, Your dear Son, our Lord, have mercy upon the unbelievers, whoever and wherever they are, who still walk in darkness, and do not yet possess the light of Your gospel. They are stricken with blindness by the evil one. Their foolish heart is darkened. They are alienated from the life that is of You, through their native ignorance, carried away to the dumb idols, even as they are led, and in their blindness curse and blaspheme Your dear Son, Jesus Christ, the mercy seat. For that reason, O faithful God, take away the veil, which is upon the heart of the Jews, who stumble at the stone of stumbling and the rock of offense. Illumine their eyes that they may know the true Messiah, the Savior of the world. Gather the heathen and all unbelievers, who look upon Your word as foolishness, into the true fold and the true assembly of Christians, the congregation of saints, so that with us and all the faithful they may honor, glorify, and worship You, the Father in the Son, and the Son with the Father and the Holy Spirit, ever one God and Lord. Open the understanding of all men, who do not put their salvation and their confidence solely and alone on the true fundament and cornerstone, even Jesus Christ, so that they know Him, whom You have sent, and in the true faith and a sincere trust of their hearts may acknowledge and accept Him as their Savior and Redeemer, who through His obedience and fulfillment of the law, and through His bitter death, has merited an eternal salvation. We ask you also, to return to the truth of Your word all of them who have defected from the Christian faith, or err in various other things and are cumbered with false doctrines. You God of grace, have mercy upon those, who are not of the true faith, who dwell in the shadow of death, and in the darkness of their minds walk on uneven paths. Seek the lost, lead aright the erring, illumine the blinded and infatuated, open the ears of the deaf, unloose the tongues of the dumb, who do not confess You, raise the fallen, bring back the corrupt, assemble the dispersed, lead aright the erring and seduced, for Your mercy’s sake. Amen.</p><p>Prayer Against False Doctrines and Sects.</p><p>Gracious God! You have warned us, that we should beware of false prophets who come in sheep’s clothing, but inwardly are ravening wolves. And since in these latter days, in which we now live, evil times will come, in which Satan clothes himself in the livery of heaven, and false teachers and fraudulent laborers dissemble as though they were the apostles of Christ, who have the form of godliness but deny the power of it; and since the defection from the pure doctrine, and the man of sin, the son of perdition, who opposes and exalts himself above all that is called God shall be made manifest, help us, eternal God, that we may be filled with the love for truth, and avoid the spirit of lies and all falsehoods and errors, abstain and flee from all appearance of evil, so that we may not be moved from the true faith, nor yet beguiled from our reward, but may continue steadfast by Your word to the end. May we never be led astray, neither through deceiving powers, signs, and miracles, nor through any temptation to unrighteousness, so that we be not tossed to and fro, and carried about with every wind of doctrine, by the slight of men, and cunning craftiness, by which they lie in wait to deceive. Preserve Your elect in this evil world, O God, that they may not be led into error. Shorten the days in these evil times. Destroy the Antichrist, the wicked child of perdition and temptation, through the spirit of Your mouth. Put an end to his days through the appearance of Your dear Son. Preserve us also from unruly spirits and schisms, from the ravenous wolves who have no mercy for the flocks, from men who speak perverse doctrines and draw the disciples to themselves, from men of corrupt minds who sow offense and bring about separation, from the tares which the enemy scatters, from thieves and murderers of the soul. Let us only hear Your voice, and follow it from our hearts in true faith and upright obedience, so that your word may be our only rule and norm, according to which we measure all doctrine, flee the evil teaching, and all unrighteousness. Then will we, too, have boldness and be not brought to nothing on that day when Jesus Christ, our Savior, shall appear. Amen.</p><p>Prayer Against the Enemies of Christendom.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ephesians 4:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 20,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 10) (1)",
        "content": "<p>O Lord God, why do the wicked rage without cause? and the mighty set themselves and take counsel against You and Your Son, Your Anointed? O Lord, how numerous Are yours enemies, and great the number of those who conspire against Your word to destroy it, and put their own evil idolatry in its place and introduce false doctrines into Your Church. They invent secret artifices and practices, to destroy the confessors of Your word. They counsel what is evil, pregnant with calamity. They lie in wait for us like the lion seeking his prey. Thus they waylay our souls. They open wide their jaws, like a gaping sepulchre, to devour us. O Lord God, commit us not to their will, for see, the wicked mob speaks in its heart, You, O Lord, have forgotten us. You have concealed Your countenance. Arise, therefore, O Lord. Lift up Your hand, forget not the distressed. Awaken, O Lord, why sleep You! Awake, and do not quite reject us. Why Do you hide Your face, and forget our misery and distress? Arise, and help us, and deliver us for Your mercy sake, that the enemy may not injure us, nor the children of Belial harm us. Save us from the hands of aliens, whose teaching is useless, and their works evil. For they will not be guided to do good. They will not regard You, the Lord of hosts, nor yet the works of Your hands. You will destroy and not build them. O Lord God Sabaoth, militate You against our assailants. Give victory to our Christian government, and conquer the enemies of Your name. For victory comes from heaven, and is not brought about by the great multitude. You can as readily help through the few as through the many. Therefore destroy the arm of the wicked. Make their counsels of no account, that they can not accomplish them, but must be brought to nothing with shame. They must return, who hate You, and seek to annihilate Your holy word. They must be as chaff before the wind. Your holy angel brush them aside, that they may not boast against You and say our hand has done this. Therefore safeguard Your honor among men, and help us for Your name’s sake, that it may not be desecrated and blasphemed. Amen.</p><p>Prayers During Times of War</p><p>Prayer During War.</p><p>O Lord God, who are righteous in Your judgment, and plenteous in mercy, whose faithfulness endures from generation to generation, who rulest even above the din of war: may all nations learn to know that You alone art God, that Jesus Christ is Your Son, and that all who truly confess Your name are the people of Your pasture and the sheep of Your hand. You have permitted the nations to rise up in war against each other and our own beloved country to become engulfed in its throes. O Lord God, our Father, we know that war is a punishment for sin and that we, too, have justly merited Your punishment through our sins. Therefore we humbly confess our sins, and supplicate Your pity and compassion, lay not our iniquities against us, but graciously forgive us our sins and shortcomings for the sake of our Lord Jesus Christ. To Your fatherly goodness and care we commend our people, and especially our soldiers and sailors now in the service of their country. They are absent from their loved ones, beset by dangers on all sides. Be You ever near. Keep them from all evil. O You, without whose consent not even a sparrow falls to the ground and who has numbered the very hairs of our heads, take them under the shadow of Your wings. Give them courage and obedience, fortitude and valor in the hour of danger, and compassion and mercy in the flush of victory. Prosper their arms to the establishment of justice, peace, and truth among all peoples. Lead them safely back to their homes and their loved ones, better citizens, better Christians than before. And to Your holy name be glory, laud, and honor, world without end. Amen.</p><p>Prayer for the Army and Navy.</p><p>Almighty and Eternal God, You King of kings and Lord of lords, who rulest and governest all things in heaven and earth: we urge Your infinite mercy to bless the officers and men of our army and navy. Preserve them against all dangers and temptations which may assail their lives. Help them manfully to battle against and overcome the powers of evil, the world, the devil, and the flesh. May they ever be filled with Your Holy Spirit from on high, and in courage, manliness, and truthfulness prevail in the hour of danger and when the battle rages. May they ever look to You, who are the succorer of those in peril, as to their only helper, and in Your name fight a holy fight to maintain the country’s honor and keep the flag unsullied, that truth and righteousness may prevail. Put to nothing all evil designs and devices of their enemies, both spiritual and temporal. Guide You and direct them that they may ever fight the good fight of faith and in the end overcome and attain to their soul’s salvation, to the glory of Your holy Name; through Jesus Christ, our Lord. Amen.</p><p>Prayer on the Eve of Battle.</p><p>O Lord God of hosts, You the Highest of the high and the Holiest of the holy, who rulest and shapest all things to the glory of Your name and the betterment of Your people: we humbly urge Your infinite mercy in this hour of our peril, judge You between us and our enemies. Be You our sure defense. Stir up Your might and hasten to our help. Lay not our sins to our charge, but for the sake of Your dear Son, our only Lord and Savior, Jesus Christ, manifest Your mercy toward us and blot out our iniquities, and sanctify and guide us by Your truth. We Your poor servants call upon Your holy name, and implore Your grace. Have mercy upon us. Lead us safely through the blood and carnage. Make it appear that You are our deliverer. Make us strong with the assurance that it is Your cause, that we are Your children, that You hold our destiny in Your hand. And if it is Your will that we should lay down our lives—Your will be done! Help us then that our last day on this earth may be the first in Your paradise. Bless our loved ones at home, and comfort them with the hopeful assurances of Your word. May they and all of us rightfully know and appreciate, whether we live, we live to the Lord. And whether we die, we die to the Lord. Therefore whether we live or die we are the Lord’s. Through Jesus Christ, our Lord. Amen.</p><p>Prayer for the Wounded.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 14:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 21,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 10) (2)",
        "content": "<p>Lord God, our Heavenly Father! We implore Your eternal compassion for all who are this day wounded, suffering, or dying. Be You near to them in their affliction. Comfort them with Your grace and with the hopeful assurance, that, though kindred and friends be far away, You are ever present and hear even the faint sight of all who seek Your succor. If it be Your pleasure, restore to them their former health and vigor. Help them to bear their pains without murmuring against Your grace. Give them patience and strength and faith in You. May they rest assured, that You will never leave nor forsake them. Deliver them from the assaults of the enemies of their souls. O God and Father in heaven, bless them and all of us, and may we ever seek those things that are acceptable to Your sight. Forgive us our sins, not because of any merit or worthiness in us, but solely because You are merciful for Christ’s sake. And to You, the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, be glory and honor forever. Amen.</p><p>Prayers for the Dying. (St. Paul’s Prayer)</p><p>“The time of my departure is at hand. I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the faith: from now on there is laid up for me a crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, shall give me at that day: not to me only, but to all of them also that love His appearing.” 2 Tim. 4:6-8.</p><p>* * *</p><p>O Lord, on Your cross You did cry, “Father, into Your hands I commend My Spirit!” I, too, commend my spirit into Your hands now when my end is near. You have redeemed me, O faithful God. Amen.</p><p>* * *</p><p>Lord Almighty God, Heavenly Father! My time has come to an end, my life is slowly ebbing away. Be You with me. O Lord, I suffer much and You only can help me. Be You my succor, and shorten and soften my pain. Darkness comes over me and I cry for comfort and strength. Have mercy upon me. Take my soul under Your protecting wing, that I perish not. Take my sins from me, and blot all my guilt; for Christ’s sake, my Savior and my Lord. Amen.</p><p>Abide with me! fast falls the eventide.</p><p>The darkness deepens: Lord, with me abide!</p><p>When other helpers fail and comforts flee,</p><p>Help of the helpless, O abide with me!</p><p>Hold You Your cross before my closing eyes,</p><p>Shine through the gloom, and point me to the skies:</p><p>Heaven’s morning breaks, and earth’s vain shadows flee:</p><p>In life, in death, O Lord, abide with me!</p><p>Prayers for the Sick and Dying</p><p>Prayer of a Patient.</p><p>Lord God, Heavenly Father! You are a faithful God, and will not suffer any one to be tempted beyond what he is able, but rather with the temptation will also make a way to escape, that he may be able to bear it. I supplicate You in my great suffering and pain, so shape the cross, that it may not lay too heavily upon me, and strengthen me that I may bear it with patience, and nevermore despair of Your mercy. O Christ, You Son of the living God! You have endured the agony of the cross for me, and have died for my sins, I urge you with my whole heart, have mercy upon me a poor sinner, and forgive me my transgressions, wherever I have sinned against You. Let my faith in no wise diminish. O God Holy Spirit! You true comforter in all times of need. Keep me ever in the spirit of patience and supplication. Sanctify me in my reliance upon You. Turn not from me in the hour of my death, and lead me from this vale of sorrow to Yourself in heaven. Amen.</p><p>Prayer for a Blessed End.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Timothy 4:6-8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 22,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 11) (1)",
        "content": "<p>O Merciful God, You have put a limit to man’s life, which no man can set aside. For he has his definite time, the number of his months rests with You. You have numbered all our days, which pass away like a stream, as though we flew away. Man is like grass, which soon withers, like the flower of the field, which passes away. Teach me, O merciful God, to know and take to heart, that I, too, must pass away and that my life has a limit, and I must go hence. See, my days are as a handbreadth before You, and my life as nothing in Your sight. Every man at his best is altogether vanity. Lord, so teach us to number our days, what it is, that we may apply our hearts to wisdom. Lord, teach me to remember that I must die, and have no continuing city in this pilgrimage. Make known to me my short and transient being, that I may often think of my end, so that in this world I may not live to myself, but live and die to You, so that I may bravely and joyously await the day of my translation and the appearance of Your dear Son, Jesus Christ, and with a consecrated life and a pious conduct hasten to Him. Bless me, O God, with a blessed departure, and when my hour comes, that I may joyously die, find a rational end in true knowledge, and that my reason and intellect be not deranged, and I speak no arrogant words or blasphemies against You, my Lord, or against my salvation. Preserve me from an evil sudden death and from eternal damnation. Let me not be suddenly overcome by my last hour without warning, but that I may prepare myself with true repentance and sincere faith. And when it comes make me joyous and brave for my temporal death, which only opens the door to the eternal life. May I then, as Your servant, depart in peace. For my eyes have seen Your salvation, which You have prepared before the face of all people; a light to lighten the Gentiles, and the glory of Your people Israel. Grant that my last word may be that which Your dear Son spoke on the cross, “Father, into Your hands do I commend My spirit!” And when I can no longer speak, hear You my last sigh through Jesus Christ. Amen.</p><p>Prayer in the Hour of Death.</p><p>Almighty, Everlasting, and Most Merciful Lord and God! You are the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, and I know that You are willing and able to fulfill whatever You have said. You can not lie. Your word is truth. You have from the beginning promised me Your dear Son Jesus Christ. And He is come, and has redeemed me from the devil, death, hell, and sin. And in Your gracious providence You have for a greater surety established the sacraments of Holy Baptism and Holy Communion in which His true natural body and blood are given me in, with, and under the bread and wine, thus offering and assuring me of the forgiveness of sins, eternal life, and every heavenly gift. And because of this Your assurance I have made use of these means of grace, and firmly relying upon Your word have received them. And now I do not doubt but that I am secure from the power of the devil, death, hell, and sin. And if now my hour has come, and this is Your divine will, I will gladly and willingly depart hence and be with You in peace. Amen.</p><p>Prayer of the Bystanders for the Sick One.</p><p>Almighty, Merciful God, who keep our life in death, We ask you, turn the eyes of Your mercy to this sick person, strengthen him in body and soul, and forgive him all his sins through Your grace. Take the sacrifice of the innocent death of Jesus Christ, Your dear Son, as a propitiation for his transgressions, since he, too, is baptized in His name, washed and cleansed with His blood. Save him from the pains and agonies of his body. Shorten his sufferings. Keep him from the accusations of his conscience and all temptations of the enemy, so that in faith he may truly battle and conquer. Grant him a blessed translation to the eternal life. Send Your holy angels, that they accompany him to the blessed company of all the elect in Christ Jesus, our Lord. Amen.</p><p>Prayer When the Patient Has Died in the Lord.</p><p>Jesus Christ, You Lord of glory and Prince of life, we glorify and thank You now and always, that You have so mercifully helped this now blessed one, and have taken his soul into Your holy keeping. And when the last day comes You would reunite his soul with his body in heavenly radiance, and grant us altogether, when the divinely appointed hour comes, to follow him in blessedness. Meanwhile grant us a Christian conduct, refresh all sorrowing hearts with heavenly solace. Keep us in Your eternal grace, for the sake of Your inexhaustible mercy and goodness. Amen. O You most holy, most glorious Trinity. Amen.</p><p>Hymns</p><p>Du Volk, das du getaufet bist.</p><p>You baptized people, one and all,</p><p>Who know your God in heaven,</p><p>Who have received a holy call,</p><p>To whom Christ’s name is given,</p><p>Do not forget, but ponder well,</p><p>The gifts that now within you dwell,</p><p>The blessings of baptism.</p><p>This washing cleanses us from sin</p><p>And lends a sacred beauty,</p><p>It makes us white and pure within,</p><p>Incites to love and duty,</p><p>From Satan’s prison sets us free,</p><p>Enables us the sons to be</p><p>And heirs of God, our Father.</p><p>Our sinful nature is renewed,</p><p>The curse of God is lifted;</p><p>By choicest blessings thus endued</p><p>And with the Spirit gifted,</p><p>We to sin are pledged to die</p><p>And by the pow’r of God on high</p><p>The gates of hell can conquer.</p><p>Here we with Jesus Christ are clad,</p><p>His righteousness receiving,</p><p>Which covers what in us is bad,</p><p>Our rescue thus achieving;</p><p>His holy blood, for sinners spilt,</p><p>Releases us from sin and guilt,</p><p>And we with God find favor.</p><p>O wondrous work, O sacred bath,</p><p>O water you of blessing,</p><p>The world nowhere your equal has,</p><p>Such healing grace possessing.</p><p>You have indeed a pow’r divine,</p><p>According to God’s own design,</p><p>And with His word connected.</p><p>You are no water such as we</p><p>Can draw from well or river.</p><p>In you the life of God we see,</p><p>Who is of grace the Giver.</p><p>His Holy Spirit in you dwells,</p><p>Who ev’ry evil lust dispels</p><p>That in our hearts would linger.</p><p>O Christians, bear this well in mind,</p><p>And thank the Lord sincerely</p><p>For all the gifts that here you find,</p><p>And that you prize so dearly.</p><p>When nothing else can soothe the soul,</p><p>These gifts lend comfort till the goal</p><p>Of life on earth appears.</p><p>Use well the things you have in store,</p><p>That are for you intended,</p><p>And, cleansed by Jesus, sin no more</p><p>Until life’s course is ended,</p><p>When you, in yonder happy land,</p><p>Before God’s radiant throne shall stand,</p><p>In heaven’s festal garments.</p><p>Paul Gerhardt, 1667.</p><p>Tr. H. Brueckner, 1918.</p><p>Ich bin getauft auf deinen Namen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 2:32"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 23,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 11) (2)",
        "content": "<p>Father, Son, and Holy Spirit,</p><p>I am baptized in Your name;</p><p>In the seed You do inherit,</p><p>With the people You do claim,</p><p>I am reckoned:</p><p>And for me the Savior came.</p><p>You receive me, O Father,</p><p>As a child and heir of Your;</p><p>Jesus, You who diedst, yes, rather</p><p>Ever live, You are mine</p><p>You, O Spirit,</p><p>Art my Guide, my Light divine.</p><p>I have pledged, and would not falter,</p><p>Truth, obedience, love to You;</p><p>I have vows upon Your alter</p><p>Ever Your alone to be,</p><p>And forever</p><p>Sin and all its lusts to flee.</p><p>Gracious God, all You have spoken</p><p>In this covenant shall take place;</p><p>But if I, alas! have broken</p><p>These my vows, hide not Your face;</p><p>And from falling</p><p>O restore me by Your grace.</p><p>Lord, to You I now surrender</p><p>All I have, and all I am;</p><p>Make my heart more true and tender,</p><p>Glorify in me Your Name,</p><p>Let obedience</p><p>To Your will be all my aim.</p><p>Help me in this high endeavor,</p><p>Father, Son, and Holy Spirit!</p><p>Bind my heart to You forever,</p><p>Till I join the heavenly host.</p><p>Living, dying,</p><p>Let me make in You my boast.</p><p>Johann Jacob Rambach, 1734.</p><p>Tr. Chas. William Schaeffer, 1860.</p><p>Wir danken dir, o Jesu Christ.</p><p>We thank You, Jesus Christ, our Lord,</p><p>For all the help You do afford.</p><p>You are the Lamb for sinners slain,</p><p>And this is our eternal gain.</p><p>Your holy supper does prepare</p><p>For us a precious fountain where</p><p>Salvation flows for ev’ry soul,</p><p>To make the wounded spirit whole.</p><p>This covenant You, Lord, did make:</p><p>“Receive my body and partake</p><p>Of my own blood which flowed for you,</p><p>That you a ransomed soul might be.”</p><p>I eat Your body, which for me</p><p>Was made to die upon the tree,</p><p>I drink Your blood and thus receive</p><p>Life for my soul, as I believe.</p><p>And though I die, yet shall I live;</p><p>Your body and Your blood will give</p><p>To me eternal life above,</p><p>The fruit of Your redeeming love.</p><p>Let me be numbered with the blest,</p><p>Who on Your loving bosom rest,</p><p>Your worthy guest, to sup with You</p><p>And praise Your name eternally.</p><p>Nicolaus Selnecker, 1572.</p><p>Tr. H. Brueckner, 1918.</p><p>Aus tiefer Not schrei ich zu dir.</p><p>Out of the depths I cry to You,</p><p>Lord, hear me, I implore You!</p><p>Bend down Your gracious ear to me,</p><p>Let my prayer come before You!</p><p>If You remember each misdeed,</p><p>If each should have its rightful meed,</p><p>Who may abide Your presence.</p><p>Our pardon is Your gift; Your love</p><p>And grace alone avail us.</p><p>Our works could ne’er our guilt remove,</p><p>The strictest life must fail us.</p><p>That none may boast himself of anything,</p><p>But own in fear Your grace has wrought</p><p>What in him seems righteous.</p><p>And thus my hope is in the Lord,</p><p>And not in my own merit;</p><p>I rest upon His faithful word</p><p>To them of contrite spirit.</p><p>That He is merciful and just,—</p><p>Here is my comfort and my trust,</p><p>His help I wait with patience.</p><p>And though it wait till the night,</p><p>And round till morning waken,</p><p>My heart shall ne’er mistrust Your might,</p><p>Nor count itself forsaken.</p><p>Do thus, You of Israel’s seed,</p><p>You of the Spirit born indeed,</p><p>Wait for your God’s appearing.</p><p>Though great our sins and sore our woes,</p><p>His grace much more abounds;</p><p>His helping love no limit knows,</p><p>Our utmost need it sounds.</p><p>Our kind and faithful Shepherd, He,</p><p>Who shall at last set Israel free</p><p>From all their sin and sorrow.</p><p>Martin Luther, 1524.</p><p>Tr. Catherine Winkworth, 1862.</p><p>Eines wuensch ich mir vor allem andern.</p><p>One thing I above all others cherish,</p><p>For one thing I long and pray.</p><p>Though in sorrow’s vale fond hopes may perish,</p><p>This will prove my staff and stay:</p><p>To see the Man who, deeply sighing</p><p>And upon earth’s bosom prostrate lying,</p><p>Drank the bitter cup of woe</p><p>Since the Father willed it so.</p><p>Ever shall my soul retain the vision</p><p>Of that Lamb, for sinners slain,</p><p>Pale and wounded, held in deep derision,</p><p>Hanging on the wood of pain,</p><p>Wrestling there in thirst and mortal anguish,</p><p>Lest my soul eternally should languish,</p><p>Being mindful, too, of me</p><p>When He died the world to free.</p><p>O Lord Jesus, keep me mindful ever</p><p>Of my guilt and of Your grace.</p><p>Be it You all bonds of sin do sever</p><p>And all blots of guilt efface.</p><p>You of night the brightest daylight make,</p><p>And Your lamb into Your bosom take,</p><p>As the Shepherd good and fair,</p><p>Nursing it with tender care.</p><p>I am Your! Will You reply by saying,</p><p>“You forever will be mine!”</p><p>Cause Your precious name, all fears allaying,</p><p>Brightly in my heart to shine!</p><p>Be with You all things begun and ended,</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Timothy 4:6-8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 24,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 12) (1)",
        "content": "<p>Who from earth to heaven have ascended.</p><p>Yes, this shall, till life is spent,</p><p>Be our law and testament.</p><p>Albert Knapp, 1829 (1823).</p><p>Tr. H. Brueckner, 1918.</p><p>Meine Seel, ermuntre dich.</p><p>Come, my soul, again inquire</p><p>If the love of Christ constrain you.</p><p>To His cross again retire;</p><p>See Him give Himself to gain you.</p><p>Search His faithfulness and try Him,</p><p>Glad in Him and gladdened by Him.</p><p>See Him there, God’s only Son</p><p>On the tree for you suspended,</p><p>Crowned with thorns, by grief undone,</p><p>Crimson stains with crimson blended,</p><p>Pierced for you, transfixed, forsaken:</p><p>Deathless love by death o’ertaken.</p><p>You, yes you had known the rod,</p><p>Endless pain your sole possession;</p><p>You had been cast off by God</p><p>For your multiplied transgression;</p><p>But the Lord your cause defended;</p><p>By His grace you are befriended.</p><p>When in mighty woe He died</p><p>Vengeance ceased and wrath abated;</p><p>Sinai was satisfied;</p><p>All things old were new-created;</p><p>Sin and death and hell were thwarted;</p><p>Life and health and heaven imparted.</p><p>Jesus, grace sufficient give</p><p>That this mind be ever in me:—</p><p>Your I am; to You would live;</p><p>Nothing from You shall ever win me.</p><p>You will not forsake nor leave me;</p><p>Let me, Lord, in love receive You.</p><p>Johann Caspar Schade, 1692.</p><p>Tr. A. Ramsey.</p><p>So nimm denn meine Haende.</p><p>Take, then, my hands, O Father,</p><p>And lead You me</p><p>Until my journey ends,</p><p>Eternally.</p><p>Alone I will not wander</p><p>One single day.</p><p>Be You my true Companion</p><p>And with me stay.</p><p>O cover with Your mercy</p><p>My poor, weak heart!</p><p>Let ev’ry thought rebellious</p><p>From me depart.</p><p>Permit Your child to linger</p><p>Here at Your feet</p><p>And blindly trust Your goodness</p><p>With faith complete.</p><p>Though nothing of Your great power</p><p>May move my soul,</p><p>With You through night and darkness</p><p>I reach the goal.</p><p>Take, then, my hands, O Father,</p><p>And lead You me</p><p>Until my journey ends,</p><p>Eternally.</p><p>Julie von Haussmann.</p><p>Tr. H. Brueckner, 1916.</p><p>Jesu, geh voran.</p><p>Jesus, still lead on,</p><p>Till our rest be won;</p><p>And although the way be cheerless,</p><p>We will follow, calm and fearless,</p><p>Guide us by Your hand</p><p>To our fatherland.</p><p>If the way be drear,</p><p>If the foe be near,</p><p>Let not faithless fears o’ertake us,</p><p>Let not faith and hope forsake us;</p><p>For through many a foe</p><p>To our home we go.</p><p>When we seek relief</p><p>From a long-felt grief,</p><p>When temptations come alluring,</p><p>Make us patient and enduring;</p><p>Show us that bright shore</p><p>Where we weep no more.</p><p>Jesus, still lead on,</p><p>Till our rest be won;</p><p>Heavenly Leader, still direct us,</p><p>Still support, console, protect us,</p><p>Till we safely stand</p><p>In our fatherland.</p><p>Nicolaus Ludwig v. Zinzendorf, 1721.</p><p>Tr. Jane Borthwick, 1846. a.</p><p>Befiehl du deine Wege.</p><p>Commit your ways and faring</p><p>With all that grieves your soul,</p><p>To His e’er faithful caring</p><p>Who does the heavens control;</p><p>Who giving course and highway</p><p>To clouds and winds and breeze,</p><p>Will find for you a byway</p><p>Where you can fare at ease.</p><p>But you must trust Him wholly</p><p>To make your welfare sure;</p><p>And look to His work solely</p><p>To have your works endure.</p><p>God lets none snatch a blessing</p><p>By fretful grief and care</p><p>Nor one’s own self-distressing;</p><p>It must be got by prayer.</p><p>Your faithfulness unfailing,</p><p>O Father, and Your grace</p><p>Mark good or ill prevailing</p><p>Among this mortal race;</p><p>Then what You will decreeing</p><p>And pressing on with ease,</p><p>You bring’st to pass and being</p><p>Whate’er Your counsels please.</p><p>All ways do Your possessing,</p><p>You lack’st not means nor might.</p><p>Your deed is purest blessing,</p><p>Your path serenest light.</p><p>And none Your work can hinder,</p><p>Nor can Your labors rest</p><p>Whenever You would render</p><p>Your children what is best.</p><p>Though devils all endeavor,</p><p>And gladly would work defeat,</p><p>Beyond all doubt soever</p><p>The Lord will not retreat.</p><p>Whatever He proposes,</p><p>Whate’er He takes in hand,</p><p>Whene’er His set time closes</p><p>Will come to pass as planned.</p><p>Hope on, poor soul, and bravely;</p><p>Hope on, no more perplexed.</p><p>God from those deeps will save you</p><p>Where you by griefs art vexed.</p><p>With grace will He secure you;</p><p>But wait His time as your,</p><p>And you will see most surely</p><p>The sun of gladness shine.</p><p>Up! Up! Away with grieving.</p><p>Bid anxious fret begone,</p><p>Your heart its sadness leaving,</p><p>Its woes ne’er dwelt upon.</p><p>Though you, who do not govern,</p><p>Can nothing at all compel,</p><p>God sits enthroned and sov’reign</p><p>And does all things well.</p><p>Let Him, a Ruler knowing,</p><p>Arrange for you, and do.</p><p>You’lt see with wonder growing</p><p>How He will bring you through;</p><p>And how, by means most seemly,</p><p>By counsels wondrous deep,</p><p>He bears Himself supremely</p><p>In works which you do weep.</p><p>True, He awhile will wait,</p><p>His comforting delayed,</p><p>And seem Himself to carry</p><p>As minded not to aid;</p><p>May seem to turn Him from you,</p><p>Nor e’en, though o’er and o’er</p><p>Your cares and griefs o’ercome you</p><p>To ask about you more.</p><p>But though you seem neglected,</p><p>And yet believing be,</p><p>When least by you expected</p><p>Will He deliver you.</p><p>Your heart will He deliver</p><p>From all its burd’ning woe</p><p>Which you, unharmed however,</p><p>Have carried until now.</p><p>And well with you thereafter,</p><p>Child of the Faithful, found</p><p>Midst praise and thanks and laughter,</p><p>A conqueror, and crowned.</p><p>The palms in hand before him,</p><p>Which God Himself bestows,</p><p>With glad songs you’lt adore Him</p><p>Who turned aside your woes.</p><p>Make end, O Lord, good ending</p><p>To all our woes before long.</p><p>With strength on us attending,</p><p>Our hands and feet make strong.</p><p>Your faithful care bestowing,</p><p>Till death, our ways attend;</p><p>Then surely, heavenward going</p><p>Our ways in heaven will end.</p><p>Paul Gerhardt, 1656.</p><p>Tr. A. Ramsey, 1916.</p><p>Alle Menschen muessen sterben.</p><p>Man is but a transient being</p><p>And a pilgrim here below,</p><p>Like a shadow, swiftly fleeing,</p><p>Like the days that come and go.</p><p>He must pass through death’s dark portal,</p><p>To obtain that life immortal</p><p>Which on yonder blissful shore</p><p>Blooms in glory forever.</p><p>So I willingly surrender</p><p>To the Lord my fleeting breath,</p><p>Knowing that His love so tender</p><p>Will allay all fear of death.</p><p>In His wounds there is redemption</p><p>And His death assures exemption</p><p>From the punishment of sin</p><p>And the sense of guilt within.</p><p>Jesus died upon the mountain,</p><p>There His precious blood was spilt.</p><p>Now an ever flowing fountain</p><p>Cleanses me from all my guilt.</p><p>Hence my soul, endowed with pinions,</p><p>Leaves for heaven’s fair dominions,</p><p>There in blissful joy to see</p><p>God, the holy Trinity.</p><p>In those fair celestial regions</p><p>All is life and peace and joy.</p><p>Souls are there in countless legions,</p><p>Happy in the Lord’s employ.</p><p>There bright Seraphim are dwelling,</p><p>Who, in majesty excelling,</p><p>Praise with heaven’s mighty host</p><p>Father, Son and Holy Spirit</p><p>There the patriarchs are living,</p><p>There the prophets all abide.</p><p>There, to Jesus homage giving,</p><p>His apostles, too, reside.</p><p>There the Lord’s whole congregation</p><p>Has a place of habitation.</p><p>There, to honor God, their King,</p><p>All their hallelujahs sing.</p><p>You city of the sainted,</p><p>Heavenly Jerusalem!</p><p>How your beauty is untainted,</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Timothy 4:6-8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 25,
        "title": "Morning and Evening Prayers for All Days of the Week (Part 12) (2)",
        "content": "<p>Sparkling like a precious gem!</p><p>What soft music there is sounding,</p><p>What sweet joys are there abounding!</p><p>Night shall never follow day,</p><p>But the sun shall reign for aye.</p><p>Ah, my eyes have seen the glory</p><p>Of that city fair and high,</p><p>And my ears have heard the story</p><p>Of those mansions in the sky.</p><p>Now the pure white robe of heaven</p><p>And a crown to me are given,</p><p>Now that light of joy is mine</p><p>Which shall never cease to shine.</p><p>John G. Albinus, 1652.</p><p>Tr. H. Brueckner, 1918.</p><p>Jerusalem, du hochgebaute Stadt.</p><p>Jerusalem, you city built on high,</p><p>Would God I were in you!</p><p>My eager spirit has so yearned to fly</p><p>And bides no more with me.</p><p>O’er hill and valley mounting</p><p>High, high o’er fields and wastes,</p><p>O’er all, all worthless counting,</p><p>Afar from earth it hastes.</p><p>O lovely day, O hour more lovely still,</p><p>When will you be revealed,</p><p>When blissfully, my voice with joy athrill,</p><p>My soul to God I yield,</p><p>His pledge ordained, unfailing</p><p>In His all-faithful hand,</p><p>That haven waits my hailing</p><p>In yonder Fatherland?</p><p>’Twill mount in e’en the twinkling of an eye</p><p>Beyond the firmament,</p><p>So wondrously, so gently laying by</p><p>Its outworn earthly tent;</p><p>Midst chariots ascending</p><p>While bright angelic bands,</p><p>Around it safe attending,</p><p>Will bear it in their hands.</p><p>All hail to you, O glorious city, now!</p><p>Lift up your gates of grace</p><p>How long the time I yearned for you, and how,</p><p>Before I reached this place</p><p>From yonder life of grieving,</p><p>Of vanity and strife,</p><p>From God at length receiving</p><p>The heritage of life.</p><p>What folk is this now drawing near to me,</p><p>This throng of worthies blest?</p><p>These, one time of th’elect on earth, I see;</p><p>The noblest crown and best</p><p>Which Christ, with grace compelling,</p><p>Sent me in other years</p><p>When still far distant dwelling</p><p>In my own land of tears.</p><p>Look, patriarchs renowned and prophets great</p><p>And gathered saintly folk,</p><p>Who while on earth endured the tyrant’s hate</p><p>And wore the Savior’s yoke!</p><p>But soaring now in freedom,</p><p>With honor clothed upon,</p><p>Midst radiant light I see them,</p><p>And shining like the sun.</p><p>Then when at length I enter in a thrill</p><p>That Paradise of God,</p><p>My every sense with rapturous joy will fill,</p><p>My mouth with praise and laud.</p><p>Hosannas there ascending</p><p>Make holy minstrelsy,</p><p>With Alleluiahs blending</p><p>For all eternity.</p><p>With instruments and with a joyful noise</p><p>Are choirs unnumbered singing,</p><p>Till by the sound and shoutings set apoise</p><p>The courts of joy are ringing.</p><p>With ten ten-thousand voices</p><p>And tens of thousands more,</p><p>The heavenly host rejoices</p><p>As ever from of yore.</p><p>Johann Matthaeus Meyfart, 1626.</p><p>Tr. A. Ramsey, 1916.</p><p>Unter Lilien jener Freuden.</p><p>Midst the lilies blooming yonder</p><p>You will wander,</p><p>Oh my soul, and be at home.</p><p>Rise, then, as on eagle-pinions—</p><p>Your dominions</p><p>Are above where angels roam.</p><p>Guide my ship, You First-born Brother,</p><p>To no other</p><p>Than that peaceful haven where,</p><p>Sheltered from all storms forever,</p><p>I shall never</p><p>Know of sorrow, sin or care.</p><p>You can fill our mouths with laughter,</p><p>And hereafter</p><p>Make our tongues to sing Your praise.</p><p>You can softly lead us mortals</p><p>Through death’s portals</p><p>And above all evils raise.</p><p>For our sins and grievous errors</p><p>All the terrors</p><p>Of the cross You did endure.</p><p>Death, your sting for aye has vanished,</p><p>You are banished,</p><p>And I rest from you secure.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Timothy 4:6-8"
        ]
      }
    ]
